WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.943 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.943 --> 00:00:03.867 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.867 --> 00:00:05.310 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.915 align:middle line:90%


00:00:08.915 --> 00:00:11.040 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Today we're going
to talk about something

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.740 align:middle line:90%
called revenue equivalence.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:25.080 align:middle line:90%


00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.873 align:middle line:84%
So so far, we've studied a
few different auction formats,

00:00:27.873 --> 00:00:30.040 align:middle line:84%
and we've solved for the
equilibria of these auction

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:30.840 align:middle line:90%
formats.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:34.480 align:middle line:84%
And we found this surprising
or peculiar finding

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.320 align:middle line:84%
that the amount of revenue
that these auctions generate

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.060 align:middle line:84%
doesn't seem to depend on
which auction format we use.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:43.460 align:middle line:84%
So I want to first review
a few of those examples.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:44.918 align:middle line:84%
And then, today,
we're going to try

00:00:44.918 --> 00:00:46.800 align:middle line:84%
to understand, in
general, why this happens

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.120 align:middle line:84%
and what are the limitations
and scope of this result.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:55.520 align:middle line:84%
So remember, we first looked
at the really simple case

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:59.880 align:middle line:84%
where we had only two bidders,
and each bidder's valuation

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.760 align:middle line:84%
was uniformly and independently
drawn from the 0, 1 interval.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:06.080 align:middle line:84%
So in the simple case,
we had v1 and v2.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:07.900 align:middle line:90%
This is bidder 1's valuation.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:09.680 align:middle line:90%
This is bidder 2's valuation.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.060 align:middle line:84%
They were in 0, 1, and they
were uniform and independent.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:19.600 align:middle line:90%


00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.240 align:middle line:84%
And in this really
simple case, we

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.005 align:middle line:84%
looked at the
first-price auction,

00:01:24.005 --> 00:01:25.880 align:middle line:84%
and we said that, in
the first-price auction,

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:26.940 align:middle line:90%
there was an equilibrium.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:30.680 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll write b
first-price auction

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:35.560 align:middle line:84%
where bidder i's bid, if
their valuation was vi,

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.580 align:middle line:84%
was half of their
true valuation.

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:39.100 align:middle line:84%
We know, in the
first-price auction,

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:43.380 align:middle line:84%
people bid in equilibrium less
than their true valuation.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:45.700 align:middle line:84%
This is sometimes
called bid shading.

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:48.200 align:middle line:84%
You shade your bid
below your valuation.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:52.240 align:middle line:84%
And we saw that this
was exactly vi over 2.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:54.880 align:middle line:84%
And then we also found an
equilibrium of the Second-Price

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.170 align:middle line:90%
Auction, so I'll write SPA.

00:01:57.170 --> 00:01:59.650 align:middle line:84%
And in the second-price
auction, we

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:04.510 align:middle line:84%
observed that the amount you
pay for the item, if you win,

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:05.710 align:middle line:90%
doesn't depend on your bid.

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:07.512 align:middle line:84%
It only depends on
other people's bids.

00:02:07.512 --> 00:02:08.970 align:middle line:84%
And we gave this
argument as to why

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:11.830 align:middle line:84%
you should always bid truthfully
in a second-price auction.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:17.970 align:middle line:84%
So here, we had bi
of vi equals vi.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:21.370 align:middle line:84%
But what we noticed
is, well, let's try

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:23.850 align:middle line:84%
to compute in each
case how much you

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:27.490 align:middle line:84%
are expected to pay in
each of these auctions.

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:31.010 align:middle line:84%
Well, in a first-price auction,
let's say your true valuation is

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:35.010 align:middle line:84%
vi and this is how
much you bid, well,

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:42.250 align:middle line:84%
your expected payment
is going to be

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:45.610 align:middle line:84%
your bid times the
probability that you actually

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:48.770 align:middle line:84%
win the good because you only
pay your bid when you win.

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:51.770 align:middle line:84%
So your expected
payment is vi over 2.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:52.630 align:middle line:90%
That's your bid.

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:55.610 align:middle line:84%
And in a first-price auction,
you pay your bid if you win.

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:59.750 align:middle line:84%
So it's the amount you pay times
the probability that you win.

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:03.290 align:middle line:84%
What is the probability
that you win in this auction

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:05.670 align:middle line:90%
if your valuation is vi?

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:08.730 align:middle line:90%


00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:12.050 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll say
this is going to be

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:13.670 align:middle line:90%
the probability of winning.

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:17.050 align:middle line:90%


00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:20.150 align:middle line:84%
Well, this was a
symmetric equilibrium.

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:22.530 align:middle line:84%
So the bidder who
wins is the bidder

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:24.130 align:middle line:90%
who has the highest valuation.

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:27.590 align:middle line:90%
So I'm going to win-- yeah.

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:28.423 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: v1, right?

00:03:28.423 --> 00:03:30.382 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Exactly, and
I guess here we have vi,

00:03:30.382 --> 00:03:31.890 align:middle line:84%
so it's going to
be vi, but exactly.

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:33.450 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, it could be 1.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:34.310 align:middle line:90%
Exactly right.

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:35.790 align:middle line:90%
Why is it vi?

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:38.490 align:middle line:84%
Well, I'm going to win
if my value is higher

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:40.890 align:middle line:84%
than everyone else's
because we're all

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:42.910 align:middle line:84%
using the same increasing
bidding function.

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:45.410 align:middle line:84%
What's the probability that my
value is higher than everyone

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:46.110 align:middle line:90%
else's?

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:48.330 align:middle line:84%
Well, there's only two of
us, so it's the probability

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:51.410 align:middle line:84%
that the other bidder
has a value below mine.

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:54.770 align:middle line:84%
But for a uniform distribution,
if my value is vi,

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:57.450 align:middle line:84%
the probability that the other
bidder's value is below vi

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:58.330 align:middle line:90%
is just vi.

00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:03.290 align:middle line:90%
This is just, for uniform, 0, 1.

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:04.410 align:middle line:90%
Here's my bid.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.210 align:middle line:90%
Here's my value vi.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:08.850 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to win if the
other bidders value lies

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:13.170 align:middle line:84%
in this interval, and the length
of this interval is exactly vi.

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:15.590 align:middle line:84%
So we multiply it by the
probability of winning,

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:17.190 align:middle line:90%
and we get vi squared over 2.

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:20.730 align:middle line:84%
This is the amount that I expect
to pay if my valuation is vi.

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:24.810 align:middle line:84%
And then we can do a
similar calculation here.

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:27.890 align:middle line:84%
What is my expected
payment in this case?

00:04:27.890 --> 00:04:31.850 align:middle line:84%
Well, we're still going
to multiply by vi,

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:33.690 align:middle line:90%
the probability of winning.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:36.890 align:middle line:84%
But now we can't just multiply
my probability of winning times

00:04:36.890 --> 00:04:39.290 align:middle line:84%
the amount I pay if I win
because the amount that I

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:42.170 align:middle line:84%
pay if I win depends on
what other people bid.

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:44.210 align:middle line:84%
So what we have
to multiply it by,

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:52.370 align:middle line:84%
is not what I pay if I win,
but my expected payment

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:53.550 align:middle line:90%
conditional on winning.

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:59.900 align:middle line:90%


00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:01.650 align:middle line:84%
And this is a general
way of breaking down

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:03.070 align:middle line:90%
how much I expect to pay.

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:06.450 align:middle line:84%
I only pay if I win, so the
expected amount that I pay

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:09.810 align:middle line:84%
is my probability
of winning times

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:11.890 align:middle line:90%
my expected payment if I win.

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:14.570 align:middle line:84%
My expected payment
conditional on winning.

00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:17.210 align:middle line:84%
Now, if I win,
how much do I pay?

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:21.530 align:middle line:84%
Will I pay the
second highest bid?

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:25.650 align:middle line:84%
If I win, the second highest bid
is the bit of the other person,

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:28.530 align:middle line:84%
and I know the other person
is bidding truthfully.

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:33.850 align:middle line:84%
So the amount that I'm going to
pay is my opponent's valuation.

00:05:33.850 --> 00:05:38.510 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out what this is
going to be is, well, let's see.

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:44.930 align:middle line:84%
It's the expectation of
maybe vj conditional on vj

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:48.050 align:middle line:90%
being less than or equal to vi.

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:50.060 align:middle line:90%
So I'm bidder i.

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:51.940 align:middle line:90%
I know that my valuation is vi.

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:53.160 align:middle line:90%
When do I win?

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:57.180 align:middle line:84%
I win exactly if my opponent's
value vj is less than mine.

00:05:57.180 --> 00:05:59.900 align:middle line:84%
And if I do win, I pay
exactly their valuation

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:02.180 align:middle line:84%
because I pay their
bid and their bid

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:04.380 align:middle line:90%
equals their valuation.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:07.780 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to get
this formula here.

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:10.540 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll erase this arrow just
to give myself some more space.

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:13.700 align:middle line:84%
So this is my
probability of winning.

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:16.780 align:middle line:84%
This is my expected payment
conditional on winning.

00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:20.360 align:middle line:84%
And can we compute this
conditional expectation here?

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:20.860 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:22.235 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yes, we
can compute it.

00:06:22.235 --> 00:06:24.580 align:middle line:84%
And actually, we can
also think about how

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:26.900 align:middle line:84%
vj is uniformly
distributed from 0

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:29.580 align:middle line:84%
to vi so that the expectation
would just be half of vi.

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:31.080 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Exactly,
let's be careful.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:33.720 align:middle line:84%
So vj is uniformly
distributed from 0 to 1.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:36.660 align:middle line:84%
I mean, you're right, but
you're saying conditional on vj

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:38.900 align:middle line:84%
being less than or equal
to vi, it's uniformly

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:40.520 align:middle line:90%
distributed on 0 to vi.

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:43.463 align:middle line:84%
And therefore,
here's the example,

00:06:43.463 --> 00:06:45.380 align:middle line:84%
if I know that the other
player's valuation is

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:48.020 align:middle line:84%
below mine, well, I know
it's somewhere in here.

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:51.320 align:middle line:84%
And if it's uniform, Well,
it turns out, conditionally,

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:52.653 align:middle line:90%
it's still uniform over here.

00:06:52.653 --> 00:06:53.320 align:middle line:90%
What's its mean?

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:55.903 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's just going to be the
middle of this interval, which

00:06:55.903 --> 00:06:57.440 align:middle line:90%
is exactly vi over 2.

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:01.300 align:middle line:90%
So exactly here, vi over 2.

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:03.800 align:middle line:84%
So if I compute this
out, what do I get?

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:07.160 align:middle line:84%
I also get vi over 2 times
vi, and what do I get?

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:10.420 align:middle line:90%
vi squared over 2.

00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:15.020 align:middle line:84%
So here, this is just a recap of
this finding we had before that,

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:17.280 align:middle line:84%
in both the first-price and
the second-price auction,

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:19.260 align:middle line:84%
at least in the equilibrium
that we looked at,

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:21.540 align:middle line:84%
the expected payment
made by bidder i,

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:25.340 align:middle line:84%
when her valuation is
vi, is exactly the same

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:26.580 align:middle line:90%
in the two auctions.

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:29.500 align:middle line:84%
And that seemed
kind of puzzling.

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:31.600 align:middle line:84%
And you wonder if
that's a coincidence.

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:37.460 align:middle line:84%
Well, more generally, let's
look at the general case.

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:45.880 align:middle line:84%
So in the general
case, the general case,

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:47.903 align:middle line:84%
we had n bidders
instead of just 2,

00:07:47.903 --> 00:07:50.320 align:middle line:84%
and we didn't assume that they
were uniformly distributed.

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:52.040 align:middle line:84%
We allowed more
general distribution.

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:55.820 align:middle line:84%
So here, we had the
valuations were v1 up to vn.

00:07:55.820 --> 00:07:59.420 align:middle line:84%
And I think what we said
is they lived in some set v

00:07:59.420 --> 00:08:08.660 align:middle line:84%
lower bar to v upper bar where
everything is nonnegative.

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:10.740 align:middle line:84%
So everyone's value
lies somewhere

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:13.980 align:middle line:84%
between this lower bound v
lower bar and this upper bound v

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:14.940 align:middle line:90%
upper bar.

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:17.240 align:middle line:84%
Up here, those bounds
were just 0 and 1,

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:19.140 align:middle line:84%
but here we're going
to be more general.

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:22.260 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to assume
that these valuations are

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:23.200 align:middle line:90%
independent.

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:26.140 align:middle line:90%


00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:30.500 align:middle line:84%
That is, the
distribution of v2 or v3

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:33.700 align:middle line:84%
is statistically independent
of the distribution of v1.

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:36.580 align:middle line:84%
But now they're drawn from
a different distribution.

00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:38.940 align:middle line:84%
Instead of the
uniform, we said they

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:46.910 align:middle line:84%
have density f over this
interval, the lower bar

00:08:46.910 --> 00:08:49.150 align:middle line:90%
to the upper bar.

00:08:49.150 --> 00:08:51.790 align:middle line:84%
And then we introduced one
more piece of notation.

00:08:51.790 --> 00:08:56.110 align:middle line:84%
It was nice to have notation for
the distribution of the maximum

00:08:56.110 --> 00:08:58.270 align:middle line:90%
of everyone else's valuation.

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:01.910 align:middle line:84%
So we looked at the
max over, let's say,

00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:07.830 align:middle line:90%
j not equal to i of vj.

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:09.930 align:middle line:90%
So I'm holding out one bidder.

00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:11.630 align:middle line:84%
I'm taking the
perspective of bidder i,

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:14.445 align:middle line:84%
and I'm thinking of all the
bidders other than bidder i.

00:09:14.445 --> 00:09:16.070 align:middle line:84%
I'm looking at all
of their valuations,

00:09:16.070 --> 00:09:17.470 align:middle line:90%
and I'm taking the maximum.

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:19.410 align:middle line:90%
This is another random variable.

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:21.050 align:middle line:84%
As bidder i, I don't
know what this is.

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:22.910 align:middle line:90%
I can form beliefs about this.

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:25.490 align:middle line:84%
And what we said is
we just had notation.

00:09:25.490 --> 00:09:28.590 align:middle line:84%
We said this has cumulative
distribution function

00:09:28.590 --> 00:09:32.725 align:middle line:90%
big G and density little g.

00:09:32.725 --> 00:09:34.350 align:middle line:84%
And we had formulas
for what these are,

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:36.690 align:middle line:84%
but the exact
formulas don't matter.

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:39.070 align:middle line:84%
It's just that we can compute
the distribution of this.

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:40.250 align:middle line:90%
And we gave a name to it.

00:09:40.250 --> 00:09:42.110 align:middle line:84%
The cumulative
distribution is big G,

00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:45.270 align:middle line:90%
and the density is little g.

00:09:45.270 --> 00:09:49.150 align:middle line:84%
Notice here that everything
here is symmetric.

00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:51.790 align:middle line:84%
So the value of i
here doesn't matter.

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:54.150 align:middle line:84%
If I'm bidder 1 and
I'm thinking about,

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:56.710 align:middle line:84%
what's the highest value
of everyone other than me?

00:09:56.710 --> 00:09:58.345 align:middle line:84%
Or if I'm bidder
2 and I'm saying,

00:09:58.345 --> 00:10:00.470 align:middle line:84%
what's the highest value
of everyone other than me?

00:10:00.470 --> 00:10:03.090 align:middle line:84%
That distribution is
going to be the same.

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:05.470 align:middle line:84%
Maybe just make sure
we're on the same page.

00:10:05.470 --> 00:10:13.190 align:middle line:84%
If n equals 2, what is
the density g going to be?

00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:16.530 align:middle line:84%
If n equals 2, so
there's two bidders.

00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:17.030 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:10:17.030 --> 00:10:17.550 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: f.

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:19.950 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Just f, because
if there's two bidders,

00:10:19.950 --> 00:10:23.970 align:middle line:84%
well, what is the maximum of
every other bidder except me?

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:25.550 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's just
the other person.

00:10:25.550 --> 00:10:27.470 align:middle line:90%
So this is just vj.

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:31.230 align:middle line:84%
And we already assumed that the
distribution of each bidder's

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:33.630 align:middle line:90%
valuation follows density f.

00:10:33.630 --> 00:10:36.350 align:middle line:84%
So in this case, g is just going
to reduce to f because there's

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:39.050 align:middle line:90%
only one other bidder.

00:10:39.050 --> 00:10:40.950 align:middle line:84%
But when n is bigger
than 2, then this

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:42.670 align:middle line:84%
is going to be a more
complicated form.

00:10:42.670 --> 00:10:44.430 align:middle line:84%
But the simple thing
is the highest bidder

00:10:44.430 --> 00:10:46.530 align:middle line:84%
among my opponents, if I
only have one opponent,

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:49.150 align:middle line:84%
is just the value
of my opponent.

00:10:49.150 --> 00:10:50.810 align:middle line:90%
OK, great.

00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:52.750 align:middle line:84%
So here was the general
case, and we also

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:54.390 align:middle line:84%
looked at the
first-price auction

00:10:54.390 --> 00:10:56.330 align:middle line:84%
and the second-price
auction in this case.

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:57.830 align:middle line:90%
And we computed equilibria.

00:10:57.830 --> 00:11:00.150 align:middle line:84%
And maybe I'll start here
with a second-price auction

00:11:00.150 --> 00:11:01.310 align:middle line:90%
because that was--

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:03.830 align:middle line:84%
maybe that's a bit
easier to write down.

00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:07.030 align:middle line:84%
So bi vi, and the second
price-auction-- people

00:11:07.030 --> 00:11:11.050 align:middle line:84%
remember what our
equilibrium was here?

00:11:11.050 --> 00:11:17.110 align:middle line:90%


00:11:17.110 --> 00:11:18.050 align:middle line:90%
I think people know.

00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:21.230 align:middle line:90%
They just don't want to say.

00:11:21.230 --> 00:11:22.850 align:middle line:84%
If we have a
second-price auction,

00:11:22.850 --> 00:11:27.230 align:middle line:84%
how much should you bid
if your valuation is vi?

00:11:27.230 --> 00:11:27.870 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:28.150 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Vi

00:11:28.150 --> 00:11:29.690 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Just Vi, you should
just bid your valuation.

00:11:29.690 --> 00:11:31.470 align:middle line:84%
So it turned out, we
gave this argument

00:11:31.470 --> 00:11:33.262 align:middle line:84%
that, in a second-price
auction, you always

00:11:33.262 --> 00:11:34.290 align:middle line:90%
want to bid truthfully.

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:36.630 align:middle line:84%
You always want to bid
your valuation, exactly.

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:39.270 align:middle line:84%
And that didn't
depend on the details

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:41.080 align:middle line:84%
of whether there's two
bidders or n bidders

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:42.330 align:middle line:90%
or what the distributions are.

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:43.730 align:middle line:90%
That's a very robust result.

00:11:43.730 --> 00:11:47.070 align:middle line:90%
So we've got vi.

00:11:47.070 --> 00:11:50.510 align:middle line:84%
And we can, again,
try to compute,

00:11:50.510 --> 00:11:54.952 align:middle line:90%
what is your expected payment?

00:11:54.952 --> 00:11:56.410 align:middle line:84%
OK, this is a little
trickier here,

00:11:56.410 --> 00:11:57.660 align:middle line:90%
but let's see if we can do it.

00:11:57.660 --> 00:12:03.110 align:middle line:90%


00:12:03.110 --> 00:12:05.750 align:middle line:84%
Well, first, let's just
write it abstractly.

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:07.950 align:middle line:90%
What is my expected payment?

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:10.450 align:middle line:84%
Well, we first
have to say, well,

00:12:10.450 --> 00:12:12.450 align:middle line:84%
there's some chance I
don't pay anything at all.

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:14.470 align:middle line:84%
If I lose the auction,
I pay nothing.

00:12:14.470 --> 00:12:16.230 align:middle line:84%
So my expected
payment, it's always

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:18.830 align:middle line:84%
helpful to break it down
into the probability

00:12:18.830 --> 00:12:22.990 align:middle line:84%
that I win the auction times
the expected amount that I pay,

00:12:22.990 --> 00:12:24.090 align:middle line:90%
given that I win.

00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:26.550 align:middle line:84%
So we'll break it down
just like we did before.

00:12:26.550 --> 00:12:30.150 align:middle line:84%
So let me first write down
the probability that I win.

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:33.670 align:middle line:84%
So this is, the
probability that I win

00:12:33.670 --> 00:12:37.160 align:middle line:84%
is the probability-- well, in
this equilibrium, everyone's

00:12:37.160 --> 00:12:38.560 align:middle line:90%
bidding their valuation.

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:40.920 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to win
if my bid is highest,

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:43.880 align:middle line:84%
which is exactly the case if my
value is higher than everyone

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:44.480 align:middle line:90%
else's.

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:48.040 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to win if the
probability of the max of j

00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:52.743 align:middle line:84%
not equal to i of vj is
less than or equal to vi.

00:12:52.743 --> 00:12:54.660 align:middle line:84%
And as usual, we don't
have to be too careful.

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:55.702 align:middle line:90%
Is it less than or equal?

00:12:55.702 --> 00:12:56.782 align:middle line:90%
Is it strictly less than.

00:12:56.782 --> 00:12:59.240 align:middle line:84%
The probability is going to be
the same, so we don't really

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:00.120 align:middle line:90%
have to--

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:02.740 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't really matter
what we write here.

00:13:02.740 --> 00:13:06.320 align:middle line:84%
So this is the
probability that I win.

00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:10.420 align:middle line:84%
Now what is the expected
payment conditional on winning?

00:13:10.420 --> 00:13:15.240 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to be an
analog of this formula here.

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:17.300 align:middle line:84%
The expected payment
conditional on winning,

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:21.220 align:middle line:84%
well it's going to
be an expectation--

00:13:21.220 --> 00:13:24.618 align:middle line:90%


00:13:24.618 --> 00:13:25.660 align:middle line:90%
a little more space here.

00:13:25.660 --> 00:13:29.320 align:middle line:90%
Let's see how small I can write.

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:31.580 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's expectation
conditional on me winning.

00:13:31.580 --> 00:13:37.680 align:middle line:84%
So I'm exactly going to win if
the max over j not equal to i vj

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:40.058 align:middle line:90%
is less than or equal to vi.

00:13:40.058 --> 00:13:41.600 align:middle line:84%
This is exactly the
event that I win.

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:45.180 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to win if everyone
else's valuation is below mine.

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:48.000 align:middle line:84%
Another way of saying that is
the maximum over everyone else's

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:51.360 align:middle line:84%
valuation is weakly
smaller than mine.

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:54.280 align:middle line:84%
And when that
happens, how much am

00:13:54.280 --> 00:13:57.280 align:middle line:84%
I going to pay in the
second-price auction?

00:13:57.280 --> 00:13:57.880 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:13:57.880 --> 00:13:59.680 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: vj.

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:01.120 align:middle line:90%
Like, the second highest.

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:03.147 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Right, and
we have some notation

00:14:03.147 --> 00:14:03.980 align:middle line:90%
for that right here.

00:14:03.980 --> 00:14:06.140 align:middle line:84%
It's exactly-- so
you're exactly right.

00:14:06.140 --> 00:14:08.120 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to pay the
second highest valuation.

00:14:08.120 --> 00:14:09.920 align:middle line:84%
But what that is
is it's precisely

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:13.100 align:middle line:84%
the highest among everyone other
than me because I'm the highest.

00:14:13.100 --> 00:14:14.100 align:middle line:90%
So you're exactly right.

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:19.000 align:middle line:84%
It is-- let's write it as this,
max vj not equal to i of vj.

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:22.040 align:middle line:84%
So it's crucial here, yes,
that this is the second highest

00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:22.900 align:middle line:90%
value.

00:14:22.900 --> 00:14:25.727 align:middle line:84%
But in the event that I have
the highest value, well,

00:14:25.727 --> 00:14:27.060 align:middle line:90%
what's the second highest value?

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:29.020 align:middle line:84%
It's just the highest
among everyone else.

00:14:29.020 --> 00:14:31.700 align:middle line:84%
So we exactly get
this formula here.

00:14:31.700 --> 00:14:36.160 align:middle line:90%


00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:42.400 align:middle line:84%
And this gets maybe
a little messy,

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:46.240 align:middle line:84%
but we're actually
able to compute it.

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:47.140 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:14:47.140 --> 00:14:54.440 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to be an integral
from v lower bar to vi.

00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:56.780 align:middle line:84%
So technically, we get into
some conditional densities.

00:14:56.780 --> 00:14:59.460 align:middle line:84%
It gets a little messy, but let
me just write out what we get.

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:01.720 align:middle line:90%
We're going to get t--

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:09.900 align:middle line:84%
maybe I'll write x, g
of x for g of vi dx.

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:16.720 align:middle line:90%


00:15:16.720 --> 00:15:21.120 align:middle line:84%
So I just wrote this
probability as g of vi

00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:25.360 align:middle line:84%
because big G is the cumulative
distribution function associated

00:15:25.360 --> 00:15:26.660 align:middle line:90%
with this random variable.

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:29.320 align:middle line:84%
So the probability that this
random variable is below vi

00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:30.970 align:middle line:90%
is, by definition, g of vi.

00:15:30.970 --> 00:15:33.410 align:middle line:84%
So here, I'm just
using a definition.

00:15:33.410 --> 00:15:35.490 align:middle line:84%
For this part, it's
a little trickier.

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:37.750 align:middle line:84%
I'm using what's called
a conditional density.

00:15:37.750 --> 00:15:40.090 align:middle line:84%
I'm saying, what is
the conditional density

00:15:40.090 --> 00:15:42.730 align:middle line:84%
of the highest value
of my opponents,

00:15:42.730 --> 00:15:46.210 align:middle line:84%
given that their
valuation is below vi?

00:15:46.210 --> 00:15:49.170 align:middle line:84%
And this here is my
conditional density.

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:52.050 align:middle line:84%
I'm integrating this
between v lower bar and vi

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:53.810 align:middle line:84%
because I'm focusing
on the event

00:15:53.810 --> 00:15:57.530 align:middle line:84%
that the highest value of my
opponents is lower than mine.

00:15:57.530 --> 00:16:00.530 align:middle line:84%
And maybe we don't need to get
too caught up in the algebra

00:16:00.530 --> 00:16:03.970 align:middle line:84%
here, but we can just see that
this G of vi and this G of vi

00:16:03.970 --> 00:16:06.390 align:middle line:84%
cancel, and we're going to
get a really simple formula.

00:16:06.390 --> 00:16:16.930 align:middle line:90%


00:16:16.930 --> 00:16:23.930 align:middle line:84%
So this is my general
formula for how much

00:16:23.930 --> 00:16:26.810 align:middle line:84%
I expect to pay in the
second price auction

00:16:26.810 --> 00:16:29.930 align:middle line:90%
if my valuation is vi.

00:16:29.930 --> 00:16:31.110 align:middle line:90%
Let's do one more.

00:16:31.110 --> 00:16:32.710 align:middle line:90%
Let's finish things off here.

00:16:32.710 --> 00:16:36.750 align:middle line:90%


00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:39.250 align:middle line:84%
And as I said, if this
conditional density is

00:16:39.250 --> 00:16:41.150 align:middle line:84%
new to you, it
won't come up again.

00:16:41.150 --> 00:16:44.350 align:middle line:84%
So the final answer is
maybe what's important here.

00:16:44.350 --> 00:16:51.490 align:middle line:84%
Let's just do one more, the
bi first-price auction of vi.

00:16:51.490 --> 00:16:52.630 align:middle line:90%
We actually computed this.

00:16:52.630 --> 00:16:55.295 align:middle line:90%
Anyone remember what this was?

00:16:55.295 --> 00:16:56.670 align:middle line:84%
We computed this
in class before.

00:16:56.670 --> 00:16:58.030 align:middle line:90%
Maybe I'll just write it down.

00:16:58.030 --> 00:17:01.730 align:middle line:84%
It turns out we had a formula
that actually looked exactly

00:17:01.730 --> 00:17:02.230 align:middle line:90%
like this.

00:17:02.230 --> 00:17:04.327 align:middle line:90%
This was actually our formula.

00:17:04.327 --> 00:17:06.410 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out we derived
this in class, I think,

00:17:06.410 --> 00:17:07.089 align:middle line:90%
last Thursday.

00:17:07.089 --> 00:17:09.210 align:middle line:84%
We used a differential
equation, and we

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:13.150 align:middle line:90%
found that it's exactly this.

00:17:13.150 --> 00:17:18.170 align:middle line:90%


00:17:18.170 --> 00:17:22.290 align:middle line:84%
So this is just a
formula that we had.

00:17:22.290 --> 00:17:24.890 align:middle line:84%
But now if this is
how much I'm bidding

00:17:24.890 --> 00:17:28.089 align:middle line:84%
in a first-price auction, what
is the expected payment that I'm

00:17:28.089 --> 00:17:30.170 align:middle line:90%
going to make?

00:17:30.170 --> 00:17:34.410 align:middle line:84%
Well, in first-price auction,
what I pay is my bid?

00:17:34.410 --> 00:17:36.930 align:middle line:84%
So my expected payment
is just my bid,

00:17:36.930 --> 00:17:39.170 align:middle line:84%
how much I pay,
times the probability

00:17:39.170 --> 00:17:41.170 align:middle line:84%
that I actually
have to pay my bid.

00:17:41.170 --> 00:17:42.710 align:middle line:90%
But when do I pay my bid?

00:17:42.710 --> 00:17:45.010 align:middle line:90%
I pay my bid when I win.

00:17:45.010 --> 00:17:53.650 align:middle line:84%
So my expected
payment, well, it's

00:17:53.650 --> 00:18:02.050 align:middle line:84%
just bi FPA of vi, my bid times
the probability that I win.

00:18:02.050 --> 00:18:03.590 align:middle line:84%
What is the
probability that I win?

00:18:03.590 --> 00:18:06.703 align:middle line:90%


00:18:06.703 --> 00:18:08.370 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's the
probability that my value

00:18:08.370 --> 00:18:09.650 align:middle line:90%
is higher than everyone else.

00:18:09.650 --> 00:18:12.230 align:middle line:84%
It's exactly this thing we
already computed down here,

00:18:12.230 --> 00:18:13.790 align:middle line:84%
and we have a simple
formula for it.

00:18:13.790 --> 00:18:14.830 align:middle line:90%
It's just g of vi.

00:18:14.830 --> 00:18:18.890 align:middle line:90%


00:18:18.890 --> 00:18:22.550 align:middle line:84%
And this is
probability of winning.

00:18:22.550 --> 00:18:25.130 align:middle line:90%


00:18:25.130 --> 00:18:29.770 align:middle line:84%
OK, well, we already have
a formula for bi FPA of vi,

00:18:29.770 --> 00:18:32.250 align:middle line:90%
so let's plug in this here.

00:18:32.250 --> 00:18:34.890 align:middle line:84%
And we see that the
G of vi cancels,

00:18:34.890 --> 00:18:37.350 align:middle line:90%
and we get the same integral.

00:18:37.350 --> 00:18:44.770 align:middle line:90%


00:18:44.770 --> 00:18:46.050 align:middle line:90%
And we get this.

00:18:46.050 --> 00:18:51.550 align:middle line:84%
So this seems like it
probably is not a coincidence,

00:18:51.550 --> 00:18:54.990 align:middle line:84%
that we've gotten exactly the
same answer in both cases.

00:18:54.990 --> 00:18:55.770 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:18:55.770 --> 00:19:00.090 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So why is bi of
vi this integral when we had

00:19:00.090 --> 00:19:03.730 align:middle line:90%
already said it was just vi?

00:19:03.730 --> 00:19:06.770 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: The key is the
superscript here, SPA.

00:19:06.770 --> 00:19:10.690 align:middle line:84%
So in a second-price auction,
the equilibrium bidding strategy

00:19:10.690 --> 00:19:13.250 align:middle line:84%
is for me to bid
exactly my valuation.

00:19:13.250 --> 00:19:16.470 align:middle line:84%
So everything we did here was
for the second-price auction.

00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:18.690 align:middle line:84%
Now we're changing cases
here-- and I should've

00:19:18.690 --> 00:19:19.830 align:middle line:90%
been more clear about this.

00:19:19.830 --> 00:19:21.780 align:middle line:84%
Now we're moving to the
first-price auction.

00:19:21.780 --> 00:19:24.620 align:middle line:84%
In the first-price auction,
the equilibrium is different.

00:19:24.620 --> 00:19:26.220 align:middle line:84%
In the first price
auction, I don't

00:19:26.220 --> 00:19:27.600 align:middle line:90%
want to bid my true valuation.

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:30.120 align:middle line:84%
If I bid my true valuation
in a first-price auction,

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:34.260 align:middle line:84%
then I can never come out ahead
because either I don't win,

00:19:34.260 --> 00:19:36.620 align:middle line:84%
or I win, but then I
have to pay my bid.

00:19:36.620 --> 00:19:38.520 align:middle line:84%
So deriving this
was a lot harder.

00:19:38.520 --> 00:19:40.860 align:middle line:84%
We spent basically all of
class deriving a differential

00:19:40.860 --> 00:19:41.420 align:middle line:90%
equation.

00:19:41.420 --> 00:19:42.920 align:middle line:84%
And in the end, we
got this formula.

00:19:42.920 --> 00:19:45.597 align:middle line:84%
So if you don't--
this is hard to see.

00:19:45.597 --> 00:19:48.180 align:middle line:84%
This took us an hour to get to,
but it should be in your notes

00:19:48.180 --> 00:19:49.620 align:middle line:90%
from last class.

00:19:49.620 --> 00:19:51.700 align:middle line:84%
So the answer to, why
are these different?

00:19:51.700 --> 00:19:56.180 align:middle line:84%
These are different equilibria
of different auctions.

00:19:56.180 --> 00:20:00.260 align:middle line:84%
But the surprising
thing is we get

00:20:00.260 --> 00:20:05.300 align:middle line:84%
exactly the same formula for the
expected payment in both cases.

00:20:05.300 --> 00:20:07.100 align:middle line:84%
And let's just see
if that's consistent.

00:20:07.100 --> 00:20:11.420 align:middle line:84%
Up here, I got vi
squared over 2.

00:20:11.420 --> 00:20:13.220 align:middle line:84%
Is this formula going
to give me vi squared

00:20:13.220 --> 00:20:14.900 align:middle line:84%
over 2 in the really
simple example,

00:20:14.900 --> 00:20:17.540 align:middle line:90%
in the simple uniform example?

00:20:17.540 --> 00:20:21.580 align:middle line:84%
Can we see this little
calculus exercise?

00:20:21.580 --> 00:20:24.260 align:middle line:84%
Well, in the uniform
example, what is g?

00:20:24.260 --> 00:20:27.500 align:middle line:84%
In our example, where we had
two bidders, and the values

00:20:27.500 --> 00:20:30.500 align:middle line:84%
were uniformly
distributed over 0 1?

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:32.820 align:middle line:84%
It's just 1 because
the first observation

00:20:32.820 --> 00:20:36.660 align:middle line:84%
that you made was that d equals
f when we have two bidders.

00:20:36.660 --> 00:20:41.100 align:middle line:84%
But if our valuations are
uniformly distributed,

00:20:41.100 --> 00:20:42.180 align:middle line:90%
what is f?

00:20:42.180 --> 00:20:43.660 align:middle line:90%
It's just 1.

00:20:43.660 --> 00:20:46.900 align:middle line:84%
So if we just plug in 1
here, we get the integral

00:20:46.900 --> 00:20:50.500 align:middle line:90%
from v lower bar to vi.

00:20:50.500 --> 00:20:53.540 align:middle line:84%
Well, so let me go
through our example.

00:20:53.540 --> 00:20:59.540 align:middle line:84%
So in our simple example,
everything works out

00:20:59.540 --> 00:21:03.020 align:middle line:90%
because g of x equals 1.

00:21:03.020 --> 00:21:06.200 align:middle line:90%
And what is v lower bar?

00:21:06.200 --> 00:21:06.700 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: 0.

00:21:06.700 --> 00:21:07.880 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: 0, right?

00:21:07.880 --> 00:21:10.100 align:middle line:84%
And v upper bar is 1,
but that doesn't show up.

00:21:10.100 --> 00:21:18.300 align:middle line:84%
So now we just get the
integral from 0 to vi of xdx.

00:21:18.300 --> 00:21:20.540 align:middle line:84%
And this is something
people should know.

00:21:20.540 --> 00:21:21.880 align:middle line:90%
This is just the antiderivative.

00:21:21.880 --> 00:21:23.860 align:middle line:84%
This is just x squared
over 2, so we just

00:21:23.860 --> 00:21:27.660 align:middle line:90%
get vi squared over 2.

00:21:27.660 --> 00:21:31.515 align:middle line:84%
And this just confirms the two
formulas that we got up here.

00:21:31.515 --> 00:21:32.640 align:middle line:90%
So everything's consistent.

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:33.460 align:middle line:90%
That's good.

00:21:33.460 --> 00:21:37.040 align:middle line:84%
And we have this kind
of surprising finding.

00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:47.780 align:middle line:84%
So note the equilibrium
expected payment

00:21:47.780 --> 00:21:56.540 align:middle line:84%
is the same in the first price
auction and the second price

00:21:56.540 --> 00:21:58.870 align:middle line:90%
auction.

00:21:58.870 --> 00:22:00.540 align:middle line:84%
To be a little more
precise, I'm talking

00:22:00.540 --> 00:22:02.560 align:middle line:84%
about a particular
equilibrium that we computed.

00:22:02.560 --> 00:22:04.282 align:middle line:90%
But I'll be a little loose here.

00:22:04.282 --> 00:22:05.740 align:middle line:84%
The equilibrium
expected payment is

00:22:05.740 --> 00:22:10.020 align:middle line:84%
the same in the
first-price auction

00:22:10.020 --> 00:22:13.340 align:middle line:90%
and the second-price auction.

00:22:13.340 --> 00:22:15.320 align:middle line:84%
And notice how this
happened came about,

00:22:15.320 --> 00:22:17.870 align:middle line:84%
getting back to the question,
the rules of these auctions

00:22:17.870 --> 00:22:19.270 align:middle line:90%
are different.

00:22:19.270 --> 00:22:22.490 align:middle line:84%
So if the players bid the same
way in these two auctions,

00:22:22.490 --> 00:22:24.430 align:middle line:84%
their payments would
definitely be different.

00:22:24.430 --> 00:22:27.660 align:middle line:84%
But the equilibrium bidding
behavior is also different.

00:22:27.660 --> 00:22:29.410 align:middle line:84%
So the rules of the
auction are different,

00:22:29.410 --> 00:22:31.050 align:middle line:84%
the bidding behavior
is different,

00:22:31.050 --> 00:22:34.150 align:middle line:84%
and somehow, these things
offset in exactly the right way

00:22:34.150 --> 00:22:37.830 align:middle line:84%
that we get exactly the
same formula in both cases.

00:22:37.830 --> 00:22:39.395 align:middle line:84%
And I think it feels,
to me at least,

00:22:39.395 --> 00:22:40.770 align:middle line:84%
that this can't
be a coincidence.

00:22:40.770 --> 00:22:42.910 align:middle line:84%
It seems like there's
something else going on here.

00:22:42.910 --> 00:22:44.750 align:middle line:84%
And what's going on
is exactly what's

00:22:44.750 --> 00:22:45.958 align:middle line:90%
called revenue equivalence.

00:22:45.958 --> 00:22:47.750 align:middle line:84%
And today, we're going
to try to understand

00:22:47.750 --> 00:22:51.410 align:middle line:84%
more generally why and under
what conditions this is true.

00:22:51.410 --> 00:22:54.290 align:middle line:84%
So I think this should
be kind of a puzzle.

00:22:54.290 --> 00:22:56.610 align:middle line:84%
It's not at all clear why
we get the same answer here.

00:22:56.610 --> 00:22:58.235 align:middle line:84%
And today, we want
to get to the bottom

00:22:58.235 --> 00:22:59.790 align:middle line:90%
and understand this puzzle.

00:22:59.790 --> 00:23:02.490 align:middle line:84%
But this was to be
motivation for what we do.

00:23:02.490 --> 00:23:04.850 align:middle line:84%
So if this is unclear, what
we do won't be motivated.

00:23:04.850 --> 00:23:07.190 align:middle line:84%
So let me pause for
some questions here.

00:23:07.190 --> 00:23:08.490 align:middle line:90%
Everything clear so far?

00:23:08.490 --> 00:23:12.030 align:middle line:90%


00:23:12.030 --> 00:23:13.150 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:23:13.150 --> 00:23:16.110 align:middle line:84%
Well, so far, we said
that the equilibrium

00:23:16.110 --> 00:23:20.258 align:middle line:84%
expected payment was the
same in these two auctions.

00:23:20.258 --> 00:23:22.050 align:middle line:84%
What if we used a
different auction format?

00:23:22.050 --> 00:23:23.350 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we could use a
third-price auction

00:23:23.350 --> 00:23:25.090 align:middle line:84%
or a fourth-price
auction, all-pay auction.

00:23:25.090 --> 00:23:27.270 align:middle line:84%
There's many different
auction formats we could use.

00:23:27.270 --> 00:23:29.550 align:middle line:84%
And we'd like our theorem
to speak to any auction

00:23:29.550 --> 00:23:30.953 align:middle line:90%
format you could come up with.

00:23:30.953 --> 00:23:33.370 align:middle line:84%
So to do that, we have to
think, well, what is an auction?

00:23:33.370 --> 00:23:36.670 align:middle line:84%
How can I define a
general abstract auction?

00:23:36.670 --> 00:23:38.050 align:middle line:90%
So let's be really general.

00:23:38.050 --> 00:23:40.290 align:middle line:84%
What is-- maybe I'll call
this a generalized auction.

00:23:40.290 --> 00:23:44.590 align:middle line:90%


00:23:44.590 --> 00:23:47.690 align:middle line:84%
And I'm still going to be
in this setting over here.

00:23:47.690 --> 00:23:51.418 align:middle line:84%
So I'm still assuming
that these assumptions

00:23:51.418 --> 00:23:53.710 align:middle line:84%
about the environment, these
assumptions about how much

00:23:53.710 --> 00:23:56.670 align:middle line:84%
each person values the item,
but what's going to be general

00:23:56.670 --> 00:23:58.890 align:middle line:84%
is the auction
format that we use.

00:23:58.890 --> 00:24:01.590 align:middle line:90%


00:24:01.590 --> 00:24:03.470 align:middle line:84%
Well, at bottom,
what is an auction?

00:24:03.470 --> 00:24:06.410 align:middle line:84%
Well, first, I have to
specify what bids are allowed.

00:24:06.410 --> 00:24:08.890 align:middle line:84%
So far, we've allowed
bids to be real numbers.

00:24:08.890 --> 00:24:10.910 align:middle line:84%
But you can have
really weird auctions

00:24:10.910 --> 00:24:13.770 align:middle line:84%
where bids can only
be multiples of 5.

00:24:13.770 --> 00:24:16.270 align:middle line:84%
Or maybe you can
only say yes or no.

00:24:16.270 --> 00:24:19.332 align:middle line:84%
Or maybe you say I really like
it, or I don't really like it.

00:24:19.332 --> 00:24:21.290 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of different
ways we could do it.

00:24:21.290 --> 00:24:23.040 align:middle line:84%
So a generalized auction
format, first, we

00:24:23.040 --> 00:24:24.570 align:middle line:90%
have to specify the bid sets.

00:24:24.570 --> 00:24:27.270 align:middle line:90%


00:24:27.270 --> 00:24:31.630 align:middle line:90%
B1, B2, all the way up to Bn.

00:24:31.630 --> 00:24:35.770 align:middle line:84%
So this is specifying what
each bidder is allowed to bid.

00:24:35.770 --> 00:24:38.395 align:middle line:84%
You could think of
these as messages.

00:24:38.395 --> 00:24:40.770 align:middle line:84%
Or there's a lot of different
ways we can think about it,

00:24:40.770 --> 00:24:43.150 align:middle line:84%
but this is just
the set of choices

00:24:43.150 --> 00:24:45.110 align:middle line:84%
that each bidder
has in the auction.

00:24:45.110 --> 00:24:50.610 align:middle line:84%
And then we need to
specify an allocation rule.

00:24:50.610 --> 00:24:53.630 align:middle line:90%


00:24:53.630 --> 00:24:55.990 align:middle line:84%
An allocation rule
says, well, if we

00:24:55.990 --> 00:25:00.230 align:middle line:84%
collect some bid by bidder
1 and some bid by bidder 2

00:25:00.230 --> 00:25:03.430 align:middle line:84%
and all the way up to a bid
by bidder n, what do we do?

00:25:03.430 --> 00:25:05.670 align:middle line:84%
Well, the auction is
to specify two things.

00:25:05.670 --> 00:25:08.310 align:middle line:84%
The allocation rule
is, who gets the good?

00:25:08.310 --> 00:25:11.200 align:middle line:84%
And then the payment rule is,
how much does everyone pay?

00:25:11.200 --> 00:25:13.520 align:middle line:84%
So we'll call it the
allocation rule and maybe

00:25:13.520 --> 00:25:15.180 align:middle line:90%
the payment or transfer rule.

00:25:15.180 --> 00:25:23.080 align:middle line:90%


00:25:23.080 --> 00:25:24.800 align:middle line:84%
Now, for instance,
so far, we've only

00:25:24.800 --> 00:25:27.600 align:middle line:84%
looked at auctions where, all
the auctions we've looked at,

00:25:27.600 --> 00:25:30.400 align:middle line:90%
you only pay if you win.

00:25:30.400 --> 00:25:32.640 align:middle line:84%
But that's actually not a
requirement of an auction.

00:25:32.640 --> 00:25:35.000 align:middle line:84%
In fact, there's something
called an all-pay auction

00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:36.440 align:middle line:84%
where everyone
submits their bid,

00:25:36.440 --> 00:25:38.520 align:middle line:84%
and you have to pay
your bid whether or not

00:25:38.520 --> 00:25:40.200 align:middle line:90%
you win the item or not.

00:25:40.200 --> 00:25:42.300 align:middle line:84%
That may sound like an
unappealing auction,

00:25:42.300 --> 00:25:44.818 align:middle line:84%
but of course, you're going to
bid less in response to that.

00:25:44.818 --> 00:25:47.360 align:middle line:84%
So we're actually going to allow
pretty general payment rules

00:25:47.360 --> 00:25:48.400 align:middle line:90%
and transfer rules.

00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:50.160 align:middle line:84%
We've generally looked
at allocation rules

00:25:50.160 --> 00:25:52.483 align:middle line:84%
where the highest
bidder gets the item.

00:25:52.483 --> 00:25:53.900 align:middle line:84%
But we could be
even more general.

00:25:53.900 --> 00:25:55.483 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we like one
of the bidders more,

00:25:55.483 --> 00:25:57.360 align:middle line:84%
and we want to always
give them the item.

00:25:57.360 --> 00:25:59.180 align:middle line:84%
So in general, what
is an allocation rule?

00:25:59.180 --> 00:26:02.160 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be a function--
maybe I'll call it q--

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:11.000 align:middle line:84%
from B1 plus Bn to,
well, it's going

00:26:11.000 --> 00:26:13.735 align:middle line:84%
to take in a profile of
bids by all the players,

00:26:13.735 --> 00:26:16.360 align:middle line:84%
and what it's going to spit out
is it should spit out something

00:26:16.360 --> 00:26:18.480 align:middle line:84%
like, I give the
good to player 1,

00:26:18.480 --> 00:26:21.745 align:middle line:84%
or I give the good to player 2,
or I give the good to player n.

00:26:21.745 --> 00:26:23.120 align:middle line:84%
The problem is
sometimes you have

00:26:23.120 --> 00:26:25.240 align:middle line:84%
to randomize because
sometimes two bidders will

00:26:25.240 --> 00:26:27.040 align:middle line:84%
bid the same amount,
and then we might

00:26:27.040 --> 00:26:29.080 align:middle line:84%
have to flip a coin to
see who gets the item.

00:26:29.080 --> 00:26:31.280 align:middle line:84%
So we have to allow
these kinds of lotteries.

00:26:31.280 --> 00:26:34.080 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to write-- it's
going to map to what I'll

00:26:34.080 --> 00:26:37.160 align:middle line:84%
call delta, where
delta is just going

00:26:37.160 --> 00:26:38.460 align:middle line:90%
to be the set of lotteries.

00:26:38.460 --> 00:26:44.560 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be the set of
all vectors q1 up to qn, where

00:26:44.560 --> 00:26:47.840 align:middle line:84%
the interpretation is, if
these are the submitted bids,

00:26:47.840 --> 00:26:50.760 align:middle line:84%
then q1 is going to
tell us the probability

00:26:50.760 --> 00:26:52.960 align:middle line:90%
that player 1 gets the item.

00:26:52.960 --> 00:26:55.280 align:middle line:90%
So these are going to be--

00:26:55.280 --> 00:26:59.500 align:middle line:84%
so q1 to qn is greater
than or equal to 0.

00:26:59.500 --> 00:27:06.560 align:middle line:90%


00:27:06.560 --> 00:27:10.280 align:middle line:84%
Now, I want to
emphasize this here.

00:27:10.280 --> 00:27:12.720 align:middle line:90%
I don't require equality.

00:27:12.720 --> 00:27:14.180 align:middle line:84%
So what's the
interpretation here?

00:27:14.180 --> 00:27:16.388 align:middle line:84%
What if I were to say, OK,
if these are the bids that

00:27:16.388 --> 00:27:18.640 align:middle line:84%
are submitted, I'm going
to allocate the good

00:27:18.640 --> 00:27:22.582 align:middle line:84%
with these probabilities that
sum to strictly less than 1.

00:27:22.582 --> 00:27:23.540 align:middle line:90%
Could this ever happen?

00:27:23.540 --> 00:27:24.740 align:middle line:90%
What's the interpretation?

00:27:24.740 --> 00:27:25.240 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:27:25.240 --> 00:27:26.990 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Well, there's
also the situation

00:27:26.990 --> 00:27:28.520 align:middle line:90%
in which no one gets it.

00:27:28.520 --> 00:27:31.840 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Right, exactly, so why
do I have to allocate the good?

00:27:31.840 --> 00:27:33.980 align:middle line:84%
I might say-- and we
see this in auctions--

00:27:33.980 --> 00:27:35.360 align:middle line:90%
no one's bid was high enough.

00:27:35.360 --> 00:27:37.380 align:middle line:84%
I'm not happy with the
bids people submitted.

00:27:37.380 --> 00:27:39.320 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to keep
the good myself.

00:27:39.320 --> 00:27:43.340 align:middle line:84%
And that would correspond to
all of these being 0, actually.

00:27:43.340 --> 00:27:47.580 align:middle line:84%
So we allow the auctioneer
to keep the good.

00:27:47.580 --> 00:27:54.400 align:middle line:90%


00:27:54.400 --> 00:27:59.280 align:middle line:84%
So more formally, 1 minus the
sum of the qi's is exactly

00:27:59.280 --> 00:28:01.412 align:middle line:84%
the probability that the
auctioneer keeps the good.

00:28:01.412 --> 00:28:03.120 align:middle line:84%
So one way of thinking
about this is it's

00:28:03.120 --> 00:28:06.220 align:middle line:84%
a probability distribution,
not over bidders 1 through n,

00:28:06.220 --> 00:28:08.583 align:middle line:84%
but over the auctioneer
and bidders 1 through n.

00:28:08.583 --> 00:28:10.000 align:middle line:84%
And it doesn't
have to sum up to 1

00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:13.200 align:middle line:84%
because the auctioneer could get
the good with some probability.

00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:15.583 align:middle line:84%
So this is just a
definition of this set delta

00:28:15.583 --> 00:28:16.500 align:middle line:90%
that I'm going to use.

00:28:16.500 --> 00:28:18.880 align:middle line:84%
So delta is the set of,
sometimes it's called,

00:28:18.880 --> 00:28:20.140 align:middle line:90%
subprobabilities.

00:28:20.140 --> 00:28:22.500 align:middle line:84%
It's like distributions
over who gets the good.

00:28:22.500 --> 00:28:25.840 align:middle line:84%
But they might sum
to less than 1.

00:28:25.840 --> 00:28:28.680 align:middle line:84%
Now what about a payment
or transfer rule?

00:28:28.680 --> 00:28:41.080 align:middle line:84%
That's going to be a
function t to Rn where,

00:28:41.080 --> 00:28:44.600 align:middle line:84%
as a function of the bids,
I'm going to specify t1

00:28:44.600 --> 00:28:47.920 align:middle line:84%
how much player 1 has to pay
me; T2, how much player 2 has

00:28:47.920 --> 00:28:52.200 align:middle line:90%
to pay me; and so on.

00:28:52.200 --> 00:28:55.920 align:middle line:84%
So just to make sure we
understand what's going on,

00:28:55.920 --> 00:28:58.380 align:middle line:84%
let's look at a
really simple example.

00:28:58.380 --> 00:29:02.730 align:middle line:90%


00:29:02.730 --> 00:29:05.530 align:middle line:84%
Let's look at the
first-price auction.

00:29:05.530 --> 00:29:08.490 align:middle line:84%
So in the standard
first-price auction,

00:29:08.490 --> 00:29:11.290 align:middle line:84%
what were these bid
sets, B1 through Bn?

00:29:11.290 --> 00:29:13.390 align:middle line:90%
What did we allow people to bid?

00:29:13.390 --> 00:29:15.990 align:middle line:90%


00:29:15.990 --> 00:29:16.490 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:29:16.490 --> 00:29:18.110 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Any real
number that's positive.

00:29:18.110 --> 00:29:20.030 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Any real number that's
positive or maybe nonnegative.

00:29:20.030 --> 00:29:21.890 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so I think
it doesn't really--

00:29:21.890 --> 00:29:24.390 align:middle line:84%
the details don't matter too
much if we allow negative bids.

00:29:24.390 --> 00:29:26.848 align:middle line:84%
But you're right, so far, we
haven't allowed negative bids.

00:29:26.848 --> 00:29:33.190 align:middle line:84%
So in that case, we had B1
is equal to Bn is equal to,

00:29:33.190 --> 00:29:35.232 align:middle line:84%
maybe I'll write, R
plus, or maybe I'll

00:29:35.232 --> 00:29:36.190 align:middle line:90%
be a bit more explicit.

00:29:36.190 --> 00:29:40.450 align:middle line:90%
I'll write 0 to infinity.

00:29:40.450 --> 00:29:43.190 align:middle line:84%
So what we said is, what is
the bid set for each player?

00:29:43.190 --> 00:29:45.753 align:middle line:84%
The bid set is just the set of
all nonnegative real numbers.

00:29:45.753 --> 00:29:47.170 align:middle line:84%
That's what we
allowed you to bid.

00:29:47.170 --> 00:29:49.450 align:middle line:84%
Maybe, in reality,
maybe you can only

00:29:49.450 --> 00:29:51.335 align:middle line:84%
bid multiples of a
set or something,

00:29:51.335 --> 00:29:52.710 align:middle line:84%
and we could add
more complexity,

00:29:52.710 --> 00:29:55.570 align:middle line:84%
but we'll just
allow this for now.

00:29:55.570 --> 00:29:59.450 align:middle line:84%
Now, specifying the allocation
rule and transfer rule,

00:29:59.450 --> 00:30:01.210 align:middle line:84%
it's just a bit messy
to write it all out

00:30:01.210 --> 00:30:03.190 align:middle line:90%
because their whole function.

00:30:03.190 --> 00:30:05.370 align:middle line:84%
So let's just go through
an example, one example.

00:30:05.370 --> 00:30:11.310 align:middle line:90%
What if, say, n equals 2?

00:30:11.310 --> 00:30:13.530 align:middle line:90%
So we have two bidders.

00:30:13.530 --> 00:30:20.450 align:middle line:84%
Let's see if we can
compute q of 3, 4 and t

00:30:20.450 --> 00:30:23.130 align:middle line:84%
of 3, 4, so really just
to make sure we understand

00:30:23.130 --> 00:30:24.610 align:middle line:90%
the notation that's going on.

00:30:24.610 --> 00:30:31.250 align:middle line:84%
So if n equals 2, the
bid set for player 1

00:30:31.250 --> 00:30:33.130 align:middle line:84%
is just a nonnegative
real number.

00:30:33.130 --> 00:30:34.850 align:middle line:84%
The bid set for player
2 is just the set

00:30:34.850 --> 00:30:36.690 align:middle line:90%
of nonnegative real numbers.

00:30:36.690 --> 00:30:39.750 align:middle line:84%
So the domain of the allocation
rule and the transfer rule,

00:30:39.750 --> 00:30:43.290 align:middle line:84%
they have to say if
bidder 1 bids this number

00:30:43.290 --> 00:30:46.570 align:middle line:84%
and bidder 2 bids this number,
who do we allocate the good to?

00:30:46.570 --> 00:30:48.810 align:middle line:84%
And how much do
we make them pay?

00:30:48.810 --> 00:30:52.570 align:middle line:84%
So when I write q of 3, 4,
the interpretation of this

00:30:52.570 --> 00:30:56.810 align:middle line:90%
is 3 is the bid by bidder 1.

00:30:56.810 --> 00:30:59.210 align:middle line:90%
4 is the bid by bidder 2.

00:30:59.210 --> 00:31:03.210 align:middle line:84%
And we need to say what happens
if bidder 1 bids 3 and bidder 2

00:31:03.210 --> 00:31:04.050 align:middle line:90%
bids 4?

00:31:04.050 --> 00:31:06.175 align:middle line:84%
And this is really just to
check our notation here.

00:31:06.175 --> 00:31:08.490 align:middle line:90%
So what is q of 3, 4?

00:31:08.490 --> 00:31:09.050 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:31:09.050 --> 00:31:10.585 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Would
it be a 0 comma 1.

00:31:10.585 --> 00:31:11.710 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: 0 comma 1, right?

00:31:11.710 --> 00:31:15.530 align:middle line:84%
Because here, because bidder
2's bid is higher than bidder

00:31:15.530 --> 00:31:18.770 align:middle line:84%
1's bid, we need to allocate
the good to bidder 2.

00:31:18.770 --> 00:31:20.350 align:middle line:90%
And we have notation for that.

00:31:20.350 --> 00:31:22.530 align:middle line:84%
Our notation just tells
us the probability

00:31:22.530 --> 00:31:24.070 align:middle line:90%
that each bidder gets the good.

00:31:24.070 --> 00:31:25.910 align:middle line:84%
Now probabilities
seem a bit weird here.

00:31:25.910 --> 00:31:26.750 align:middle line:90%
They're just 0, 1.

00:31:26.750 --> 00:31:28.730 align:middle line:84%
We always give the
good to bidder 2.

00:31:28.730 --> 00:31:30.590 align:middle line:90%
If bidder 2's bid is higher.

00:31:30.590 --> 00:31:32.810 align:middle line:84%
So this is just going
to be the vector 0 comma

00:31:32.810 --> 00:31:37.810 align:middle line:84%
1 that says we give the good
to bidder 1 with probability 0,

00:31:37.810 --> 00:31:42.950 align:middle line:84%
and we give the good to
bidder 2 with probability 1.

00:31:42.950 --> 00:31:48.330 align:middle line:84%
What about the
vector t, t of 3, 4.

00:31:48.330 --> 00:31:49.550 align:middle line:90%
What is that going to be?

00:31:49.550 --> 00:31:50.050 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:31:50.050 --> 00:31:50.770 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: 0, 4.

00:31:50.770 --> 00:31:51.470 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: 0, 4.

00:31:51.470 --> 00:31:55.235 align:middle line:90%


00:31:55.235 --> 00:31:58.380 align:middle line:84%
This says, well,
this first component

00:31:58.380 --> 00:32:00.140 align:middle line:90%
we might call t1 of 3, 4.

00:32:00.140 --> 00:32:01.967 align:middle line:84%
This is how much
bidder 1 has to pay.

00:32:01.967 --> 00:32:04.300 align:middle line:84%
Well, bidder 1 got outbid,
they're not getting the item,

00:32:04.300 --> 00:32:05.940 align:middle line:90%
and therefore they don't pay.

00:32:05.940 --> 00:32:07.500 align:middle line:90%
So they pay 0.

00:32:07.500 --> 00:32:10.390 align:middle line:90%
Bidder 2 was the highest bidder.

00:32:10.390 --> 00:32:12.140 align:middle line:84%
They're winning the
item, and in the rules

00:32:12.140 --> 00:32:15.260 align:middle line:84%
of the first-price auction,
they pay their bid.

00:32:15.260 --> 00:32:17.580 align:middle line:90%
So we get 0, 4.

00:32:17.580 --> 00:32:21.260 align:middle line:84%
Now, what if I were to change
this to a second-price auction?

00:32:21.260 --> 00:32:23.300 align:middle line:84%
If I change this to a
second-price auction,

00:32:23.300 --> 00:32:24.520 align:middle line:90%
what would change here?

00:32:24.520 --> 00:32:25.255 align:middle line:90%
Would q change?

00:32:25.255 --> 00:32:25.880 align:middle line:90%
Would t change?

00:32:25.880 --> 00:32:29.060 align:middle line:90%
What would be different?

00:32:29.060 --> 00:32:29.700 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:32:29.700 --> 00:32:31.242 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: q would
not change because,

00:32:31.242 --> 00:32:33.480 align:middle line:90%
still, the person bidding 4--

00:32:33.480 --> 00:32:34.580 align:middle line:90%
well, yeah, they'll bid 4.

00:32:34.580 --> 00:32:35.580 align:middle line:90%
In this case, would win.

00:32:35.580 --> 00:32:38.363 align:middle line:84%
T would change, however, because
they would only have to pay 3.

00:32:38.363 --> 00:32:39.780 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Exactly,
so notice, when

00:32:39.780 --> 00:32:41.888 align:middle line:84%
we compare the first-
and second-price auction,

00:32:41.888 --> 00:32:43.680 align:middle line:84%
they actually use the
same allocation rule.

00:32:43.680 --> 00:32:45.280 align:middle line:84%
And a lot of auctions
use this allocation rule.

00:32:45.280 --> 00:32:47.488 align:middle line:84%
The allocation rule is pretty
simple-- we allocate it

00:32:47.488 --> 00:32:48.700 align:middle line:90%
to the highest bidder.

00:32:48.700 --> 00:32:50.600 align:middle line:84%
They both use that
allocation rule.

00:32:50.600 --> 00:32:52.540 align:middle line:84%
The difference between
the first-price auction

00:32:52.540 --> 00:32:54.860 align:middle line:84%
and the second-price
auction is the payment rule,

00:32:54.860 --> 00:32:56.180 align:middle line:90%
how much people have to pay.

00:32:56.180 --> 00:32:59.980 align:middle line:84%
And indeed, down here,
what we would get is 0, 3.

00:32:59.980 --> 00:33:01.740 align:middle line:84%
If it were a
second-price auction,

00:33:01.740 --> 00:33:03.640 align:middle line:90%
the first bidder got outbid.

00:33:03.640 --> 00:33:06.740 align:middle line:84%
They still lose the
item and pay nothing.

00:33:06.740 --> 00:33:08.800 align:middle line:84%
The second bidder, in
a second-price auction,

00:33:08.800 --> 00:33:12.500 align:middle line:84%
though, pays the-- well,
bidder 2 is the highest bidder.

00:33:12.500 --> 00:33:14.160 align:middle line:90%
They pay the second highest bid.

00:33:14.160 --> 00:33:16.520 align:middle line:84%
Well, what's the second
highest bid between 3 and 4?

00:33:16.520 --> 00:33:17.500 align:middle line:90%
It would just be 3.

00:33:17.500 --> 00:33:19.380 align:middle line:84%
So we'd have to
change this 4 to a 3

00:33:19.380 --> 00:33:22.230 align:middle line:84%
if we wanted to do the
first-price auction.

00:33:22.230 --> 00:33:23.480 align:middle line:90%
What about an all-pay auction?

00:33:23.480 --> 00:33:25.522 align:middle line:84%
What would this be if it
were an all-pay auction?

00:33:25.522 --> 00:33:28.430 align:middle line:90%


00:33:28.430 --> 00:33:31.580 align:middle line:84%
Well, then it would just be
3, 4 because bidder 1 would

00:33:31.580 --> 00:33:34.420 align:middle line:84%
have to pay 3 even though they
didn't when the good and bidder

00:33:34.420 --> 00:33:35.400 align:middle line:90%
2 would pay 4.

00:33:35.400 --> 00:33:36.460 align:middle line:90%
They'd pay their own bid.

00:33:36.460 --> 00:33:39.220 align:middle line:84%
OK, so I think we understand
the notation here.

00:33:39.220 --> 00:33:41.960 align:middle line:84%
And now that we have this
generalized auction format,

00:33:41.960 --> 00:33:46.860 align:middle line:84%
we want to try to understand
these expected payments.

00:33:46.860 --> 00:33:50.380 align:middle line:84%
Now, to talk about how
much people expect to pay,

00:33:50.380 --> 00:33:52.400 align:middle line:84%
we have to first have
an equilibrium in mind.

00:33:52.400 --> 00:33:56.018 align:middle line:84%
This is a key conceptual
point that, when I say--

00:33:56.018 --> 00:33:57.560 align:middle line:84%
notice when I said
expected payments,

00:33:57.560 --> 00:33:59.260 align:middle line:84%
I said equilibrium-expected
payments

00:33:59.260 --> 00:34:01.482 align:middle line:84%
because, just from the
auction format alone,

00:34:01.482 --> 00:34:02.940 align:middle line:84%
we have no idea
how much people are

00:34:02.940 --> 00:34:05.860 align:middle line:84%
going to pay until we specify
how people actually bid.

00:34:05.860 --> 00:34:09.219 align:middle line:84%
So first, we need
to choose or focus

00:34:09.219 --> 00:34:11.560 align:middle line:84%
our attention on an
equilibrium of the auction.

00:34:11.560 --> 00:34:13.199 align:middle line:84%
And then, given
that equilibrium,

00:34:13.199 --> 00:34:16.139 align:middle line:84%
we can compute how much
people are expected to pay.

00:34:16.139 --> 00:34:22.820 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
consider an equilibrium,

00:34:22.820 --> 00:34:28.340 align:middle line:84%
a Bayes-Nash equilibrium of
some generalized auction.

00:34:28.340 --> 00:34:31.219 align:middle line:84%
And let's just make sure we
understand what kind of object

00:34:31.219 --> 00:34:32.380 align:middle line:90%
this is.

00:34:32.380 --> 00:34:34.580 align:middle line:84%
So a Bayes-Nash
equilibrium is going

00:34:34.580 --> 00:34:39.620 align:middle line:84%
to specify
functions-- let's see.

00:34:39.620 --> 00:34:41.100 align:middle line:84%
Usually, we use
star, but I think

00:34:41.100 --> 00:34:42.260 align:middle line:84%
I'm actually going
to use hats today

00:34:42.260 --> 00:34:43.960 align:middle line:84%
because we're going to have
to take some derivatives,

00:34:43.960 --> 00:34:45.739 align:middle line:84%
and with the stars,
it's a bit messy.

00:34:45.739 --> 00:34:48.420 align:middle line:84%
So bidder 1's
equilibrium strategy

00:34:48.420 --> 00:34:52.300 align:middle line:84%
is going to be a
mapping from v lower

00:34:52.300 --> 00:34:58.680 align:middle line:84%
bar to v upper bar to B1,
all the way down to Bn hat.

00:34:58.680 --> 00:35:05.740 align:middle line:90%


00:35:05.740 --> 00:35:08.260 align:middle line:84%
So remember, Bayes-Nash
equilibrium is a strategy

00:35:08.260 --> 00:35:10.740 align:middle line:84%
profile, and a
strategy profile just

00:35:10.740 --> 00:35:13.300 align:middle line:84%
specifies a strategy or a
bidding function for each

00:35:13.300 --> 00:35:14.700 align:middle line:90%
of the players.

00:35:14.700 --> 00:35:17.140 align:middle line:84%
Notice here that, in
general, these bid sets

00:35:17.140 --> 00:35:20.120 align:middle line:84%
could be different, and our
strategies have to reflect that.

00:35:20.120 --> 00:35:22.340 align:middle line:90%
So what does bidder 1 do?

00:35:22.340 --> 00:35:24.420 align:middle line:84%
They know what
their true value is.

00:35:24.420 --> 00:35:27.060 align:middle line:84%
It's somewhere between v
lower bar and v upper bar.

00:35:27.060 --> 00:35:29.180 align:middle line:84%
And to each true value,
their bidding function

00:35:29.180 --> 00:35:31.220 align:middle line:90%
specifies how much they bid.

00:35:31.220 --> 00:35:32.420 align:middle line:90%
Well, what is a bid?

00:35:32.420 --> 00:35:34.425 align:middle line:84%
A bid is just something
in the set B1.

00:35:34.425 --> 00:35:35.800 align:middle line:84%
Remember this is
really abstract.

00:35:35.800 --> 00:35:38.940 align:middle line:84%
B1 could be, I like
it, or I don't like it.

00:35:38.940 --> 00:35:41.460 align:middle line:84%
And then my strategy would
say, for which values,

00:35:41.460 --> 00:35:43.080 align:middle line:84%
do I tell the
auctioneer, I like it?

00:35:43.080 --> 00:35:46.100 align:middle line:84%
And for which values, do I tell
the auctioneer, I don't like it?

00:35:46.100 --> 00:35:49.510 align:middle line:84%
And then, all the way down to
bidder n, whose bidding function

00:35:49.510 --> 00:35:51.510 align:middle line:84%
takes each value they
could possibly have

00:35:51.510 --> 00:35:53.667 align:middle line:84%
and assigns it to some
bid that's feasible,

00:35:53.667 --> 00:35:55.750 align:middle line:84%
some bid that's in the bid
set that the auctioneer

00:35:55.750 --> 00:35:57.830 align:middle line:90%
has presented.

00:35:57.830 --> 00:36:02.750 align:middle line:84%
OK, so we're considering
a Bayes-Nash equilibrium,

00:36:02.750 --> 00:36:05.850 align:middle line:84%
and what we're interested
in is the payments.

00:36:05.850 --> 00:36:09.130 align:middle line:84%
We're interested in the expected
payment that each bidder makes.

00:36:09.130 --> 00:36:11.630 align:middle line:84%
And we might also be interested
in how likely each bidder is

00:36:11.630 --> 00:36:13.030 align:middle line:90%
to win the good.

00:36:13.030 --> 00:36:18.350 align:middle line:84%
So given this, we
want to define what

00:36:18.350 --> 00:36:26.190 align:middle line:84%
we might call the
equilibrium-expected allocation

00:36:26.190 --> 00:36:27.010 align:middle line:90%
and payments.

00:36:27.010 --> 00:36:34.390 align:middle line:90%


00:36:34.390 --> 00:36:39.290 align:middle line:84%
So let's first define
the allocation.

00:36:39.290 --> 00:36:44.790 align:middle line:90%
Maybe I'll call it Qi hat.

00:36:44.790 --> 00:36:45.825 align:middle line:90%
Let's focus on bidder 1.

00:36:45.825 --> 00:36:47.950 align:middle line:84%
I think it just makes the
notation a little easier.

00:36:47.950 --> 00:36:50.690 align:middle line:90%
Q1 hat of v1.

00:36:50.690 --> 00:36:54.510 align:middle line:90%


00:36:54.510 --> 00:36:56.150 align:middle line:84%
So the hat reminds
us that this depends

00:36:56.150 --> 00:36:58.030 align:middle line:90%
on the equilibrium v1 hat.

00:36:58.030 --> 00:36:59.950 align:middle line:84%
And what I want to
compute here is--

00:36:59.950 --> 00:37:03.510 align:middle line:84%
let's say we're playing
this generalized auction,

00:37:03.510 --> 00:37:06.870 align:middle line:84%
and we're following this
Bayes-Nash equilibrium.

00:37:06.870 --> 00:37:08.910 align:middle line:90%
Suppose I'm bidder 1.

00:37:08.910 --> 00:37:12.030 align:middle line:84%
Suppose my true valuation
for the good is v1.

00:37:12.030 --> 00:37:17.550 align:middle line:84%
What is my probability or
my expected probability

00:37:17.550 --> 00:37:20.590 align:middle line:90%
of winning the item?

00:37:20.590 --> 00:37:23.670 align:middle line:84%
Well, whether I win the
item depends on what

00:37:23.670 --> 00:37:25.130 align:middle line:90%
other people's valuations are.

00:37:25.130 --> 00:37:27.310 align:middle line:84%
So I need to take
an expectation.

00:37:27.310 --> 00:37:33.150 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to take
the expectation of--

00:37:33.150 --> 00:37:36.670 align:middle line:90%


00:37:36.670 --> 00:37:38.090 align:middle line:90%
well, let's write this.

00:37:38.090 --> 00:37:48.245 align:middle line:90%


00:37:48.245 --> 00:37:50.370 align:middle line:84%
Let's make sure we understand
what's going on here.

00:37:50.370 --> 00:37:54.110 align:middle line:90%


00:37:54.110 --> 00:38:01.350 align:middle line:84%
And I'll say this
expectation is over v2 to vn.

00:38:01.350 --> 00:38:06.070 align:middle line:84%
Why do I not take
expectations over v1?

00:38:06.070 --> 00:38:07.130 align:middle line:90%
Well, I know v1.

00:38:07.130 --> 00:38:07.790 align:middle line:90%
I'm bidder 1.

00:38:07.790 --> 00:38:08.730 align:middle line:90%
I know what V1 is.

00:38:08.730 --> 00:38:09.590 align:middle line:84%
So this is what's,
I think, really

00:38:09.590 --> 00:38:10.990 align:middle line:90%
tricky about this notation.

00:38:10.990 --> 00:38:13.630 align:middle line:84%
When you see v1 in here,
that's just a number.

00:38:13.630 --> 00:38:15.030 align:middle line:90%
That's something I know.

00:38:15.030 --> 00:38:17.110 align:middle line:84%
When you see vn, that's
something I don't know,

00:38:17.110 --> 00:38:18.630 align:middle line:84%
and that's something
that's random.

00:38:18.630 --> 00:38:19.885 align:middle line:90%
So think of v1.

00:38:19.885 --> 00:38:21.010 align:middle line:90%
It's just something like 2.

00:38:21.010 --> 00:38:21.770 align:middle line:90%
It's a number.

00:38:21.770 --> 00:38:24.030 align:middle line:84%
We're not taking an
expectation over it,

00:38:24.030 --> 00:38:26.970 align:middle line:84%
but everyone else's bid--
everyone else's value,

00:38:26.970 --> 00:38:28.470 align:middle line:84%
we are taking an
expectation over it

00:38:28.470 --> 00:38:30.390 align:middle line:84%
because we don't
know what it is.

00:38:30.390 --> 00:38:36.870 align:middle line:84%
So what this is saying is if my
value is v1 as bidder 1, well,

00:38:36.870 --> 00:38:40.470 align:middle line:84%
then the amount I'm going to bid
in equilibrium is B1 hat of v1.

00:38:40.470 --> 00:38:42.800 align:middle line:84%
That's what my equilibrium
bidding strategy tells me

00:38:42.800 --> 00:38:44.440 align:middle line:90%
I should bid.

00:38:44.440 --> 00:38:46.360 align:middle line:84%
Now let's take the
example of bidder n.

00:38:46.360 --> 00:38:49.560 align:middle line:84%
Well, I don't know what
bidder n's value is,

00:38:49.560 --> 00:38:52.760 align:middle line:84%
but if their value is vn,
then how much do they bid?

00:38:52.760 --> 00:38:55.440 align:middle line:90%
They bid vn hat of vn.

00:38:55.440 --> 00:38:58.200 align:middle line:84%
And we'll do the same for bidder
n minus 1 and bidder n minus 3,

00:38:58.200 --> 00:39:00.000 align:middle line:90%
all the way down to bidder 2.

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:05.520 align:middle line:84%
So what's in here is
the profile of bids

00:39:05.520 --> 00:39:07.880 align:middle line:84%
that are submitted when we're
following the equilibrium

00:39:07.880 --> 00:39:08.740 align:middle line:90%
strategy.

00:39:08.740 --> 00:39:10.680 align:middle line:84%
And bidder 1's
value is v1, bidder

00:39:10.680 --> 00:39:15.000 align:middle line:84%
2's value is V2 all the way
up to bidder n's value is vn.

00:39:15.000 --> 00:39:18.840 align:middle line:84%
Now, bidder 1 knows
her own value, v1,

00:39:18.840 --> 00:39:21.240 align:middle line:84%
but she doesn't know
these other values.

00:39:21.240 --> 00:39:23.200 align:middle line:84%
So that's why we
take an expectation.

00:39:23.200 --> 00:39:28.120 align:middle line:84%
And then notice we have to
feed in this profile of bids

00:39:28.120 --> 00:39:29.798 align:middle line:90%
into our allocation function.

00:39:29.798 --> 00:39:31.340 align:middle line:84%
And I realized I
made a mistake here.

00:39:31.340 --> 00:39:33.000 align:middle line:90%
We need a 1 here.

00:39:33.000 --> 00:39:39.680 align:middle line:84%
Should be q1 because this
is the profile of bids.

00:39:39.680 --> 00:39:43.720 align:middle line:84%
q1 of this profile is going
to specify the probability

00:39:43.720 --> 00:39:47.120 align:middle line:84%
that player 1-- or bidder 1
gets the item when this is

00:39:47.120 --> 00:39:50.037 align:middle line:90%
the submitted profile of bids.

00:39:50.037 --> 00:39:51.620 align:middle line:84%
And then we compute
expectations here.

00:39:51.620 --> 00:39:53.203 align:middle line:84%
But I think this is
a little abstract.

00:39:53.203 --> 00:39:55.240 align:middle line:90%
So clear-- yeah.

00:39:55.240 --> 00:39:59.120 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So player 1 can't know
her own allocation function,

00:39:59.120 --> 00:40:00.620 align:middle line:90%
right?

00:40:00.620 --> 00:40:03.693 align:middle line:84%
Because it depends on
other people's values.

00:40:03.693 --> 00:40:04.860 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: So let's be clear.

00:40:04.860 --> 00:40:06.200 align:middle line:84%
There's a key difference
here between the function

00:40:06.200 --> 00:40:07.620 align:middle line:90%
and the value of the function.

00:40:07.620 --> 00:40:09.640 align:middle line:84%
So she does know
the allocation rule.

00:40:09.640 --> 00:40:12.280 align:middle line:84%
She knows if these are the
bids that are submitted,

00:40:12.280 --> 00:40:14.023 align:middle line:84%
then this is the amount
I'm going to get.

00:40:14.023 --> 00:40:15.440 align:middle line:84%
But you're right,
she doesn't know

00:40:15.440 --> 00:40:17.773 align:middle line:84%
the bids that are actually
submitted because she doesn't

00:40:17.773 --> 00:40:18.920 align:middle line:90%
know how other people bid.

00:40:18.920 --> 00:40:21.680 align:middle line:84%
So this thing in
here, she doesn't

00:40:21.680 --> 00:40:23.200 align:middle line:90%
know what value this takes.

00:40:23.200 --> 00:40:26.600 align:middle line:84%
The value this takes is going
to depend on these bids here.

00:40:26.600 --> 00:40:28.740 align:middle line:84%
But she's able to form
beliefs over that.

00:40:28.740 --> 00:40:31.500 align:middle line:84%
She's able to say things
like, well, I don't know--

00:40:31.500 --> 00:40:32.900 align:middle line:90%
let's say there's two bidders--

00:40:32.900 --> 00:40:34.880 align:middle line:84%
I don't know what
bidder 2's going to bid,

00:40:34.880 --> 00:40:38.260 align:middle line:84%
but if bidder 2 bids 5, this is
the probability I get the good.

00:40:38.260 --> 00:40:40.980 align:middle line:84%
If bidder 2 bids 7, this is the
probability that I get the good.

00:40:40.980 --> 00:40:43.600 align:middle line:84%
And because she has a belief
about the distribution

00:40:43.600 --> 00:40:46.160 align:middle line:84%
from which bidder 2's
valuations are drawn

00:40:46.160 --> 00:40:49.040 align:middle line:84%
and she believes that bidder 2's
following bidder 2's equilibrium

00:40:49.040 --> 00:40:51.740 align:middle line:84%
strategy, she's able to
compute an expectation here.

00:40:51.740 --> 00:40:54.120 align:middle line:90%
So she doesn't know the actual--

00:40:54.120 --> 00:40:58.880 align:middle line:84%
I guess what's also tricky
here, it's weird to talk about--

00:40:58.880 --> 00:41:00.655 align:middle line:84%
this is a probability
on the inside.

00:41:00.655 --> 00:41:02.780 align:middle line:84%
And then we're also taking
an expected probability.

00:41:02.780 --> 00:41:04.560 align:middle line:84%
I know that's a little
weird, but think

00:41:04.560 --> 00:41:07.420 align:middle line:84%
of this number as basically
always being 0, 1 on the inside.

00:41:07.420 --> 00:41:09.640 align:middle line:84%
So we're basically saying,
what's the probability

00:41:09.640 --> 00:41:10.800 align:middle line:90%
that I get the good?

00:41:10.800 --> 00:41:12.800 align:middle line:84%
So we're taking an
expectation of something

00:41:12.800 --> 00:41:15.240 align:middle line:84%
that's sometimes 1--
it's 1 if I win--

00:41:15.240 --> 00:41:17.300 align:middle line:84%
and sometimes 0--
it's 0 if I lose.

00:41:17.300 --> 00:41:20.747 align:middle line:84%
This tie-breaking stuff is
not really going to come up.

00:41:20.747 --> 00:41:21.580 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

00:41:21.580 --> 00:41:25.040 align:middle line:90%
Great, great question, OK.

00:41:25.040 --> 00:41:28.080 align:middle line:84%
Great, so this is the
equilibrium probability

00:41:28.080 --> 00:41:32.193 align:middle line:84%
that I get the good if I'm
bidder 1 and my valuation is v1.

00:41:32.193 --> 00:41:33.860 align:middle line:84%
Now let's do the same
with the transfer.

00:41:33.860 --> 00:41:37.360 align:middle line:84%
So we wanted to
find T1 hat of v1.

00:41:37.360 --> 00:41:40.060 align:middle line:84%
So this is exactly the object
that we are computing over here.

00:41:40.060 --> 00:41:42.240 align:middle line:84%
We are saying if I'm
bidder v1-- if I'm

00:41:42.240 --> 00:41:44.458 align:middle line:84%
bidder 1 and my
valuation is v1, what

00:41:44.458 --> 00:41:46.000 align:middle line:84%
is the expected
amount that I'm going

00:41:46.000 --> 00:41:48.040 align:middle line:84%
to pay in this
particular auction,

00:41:48.040 --> 00:41:50.320 align:middle line:84%
in this particular
equilibrium of the auction.

00:41:50.320 --> 00:41:53.080 align:middle line:84%
So again, we're going
to take expectations.

00:41:53.080 --> 00:41:56.440 align:middle line:84%
Again, our expectations are
going to be over v2 through vn

00:41:56.440 --> 00:42:00.560 align:middle line:84%
because those are the values
that bidder 1 does not know.

00:42:00.560 --> 00:42:07.320 align:middle line:84%
And now we're going to write
T1 of b1 hat v1 all the way up

00:42:07.320 --> 00:42:12.320 align:middle line:90%
to bn hat vn here.

00:42:12.320 --> 00:42:14.600 align:middle line:84%
And maybe I'll make
it clear that this

00:42:14.600 --> 00:42:22.040 align:middle line:84%
is known where these are
uncertain from the perspective

00:42:22.040 --> 00:42:25.320 align:middle line:90%
of bidder 1.

00:42:25.320 --> 00:42:30.120 align:middle line:84%
So we have bidder 1's bid all
the way up to bidder n's bid.

00:42:30.120 --> 00:42:32.200 align:middle line:90%
That forms the profile of bids.

00:42:32.200 --> 00:42:35.070 align:middle line:84%
The auctioneer says, if I
get this profile of bids,

00:42:35.070 --> 00:42:37.610 align:middle line:84%
this is how much I'm going
to ask player 1 to pay.

00:42:37.610 --> 00:42:40.570 align:middle line:84%
And then we're
taking expectations.

00:42:40.570 --> 00:42:44.450 align:middle line:84%
So we've now kind of
formalized our question.

00:42:44.450 --> 00:42:47.290 align:middle line:84%
Revenue equivalence tells
us that this function,

00:42:47.290 --> 00:42:50.650 align:middle line:84%
T1 hat of v1, seems
to take the same form

00:42:50.650 --> 00:42:52.630 align:middle line:84%
in a lot of different
auction formats

00:42:52.630 --> 00:42:54.450 align:middle line:84%
and a lot of
different equilibria.

00:42:54.450 --> 00:42:56.770 align:middle line:84%
In both the equilibrium
of the first-price auction

00:42:56.770 --> 00:42:59.130 align:middle line:84%
and the equilibrium of
the second-price auction,

00:42:59.130 --> 00:43:00.530 align:middle line:90%
this function was the same.

00:43:00.530 --> 00:43:02.810 align:middle line:84%
And we'd like to
understand why that is.

00:43:02.810 --> 00:43:04.930 align:middle line:84%
So that's at a high
level what we want to.

00:43:04.930 --> 00:43:08.050 align:middle line:90%


00:43:08.050 --> 00:43:11.290 align:middle line:84%
OK, well, we're going to have
to use the fact that this

00:43:11.290 --> 00:43:14.770 align:middle line:90%
is a Bayes-Nash equilibrium.

00:43:14.770 --> 00:43:19.250 align:middle line:84%
So the fact that this is
Bayes-Nash equilibrium tells me

00:43:19.250 --> 00:43:19.790 align:middle line:90%
that--

00:43:19.790 --> 00:43:26.610 align:middle line:90%


00:43:26.610 --> 00:43:30.930 align:middle line:84%
let's take the
perspective of bidder 1

00:43:30.930 --> 00:43:32.390 align:middle line:90%
who has a valuation of v1?

00:43:32.390 --> 00:43:38.610 align:middle line:90%


00:43:38.610 --> 00:43:51.150 align:middle line:84%
What we know is that bidding
b1 hat of v1 is optimal.

00:43:51.150 --> 00:43:53.850 align:middle line:90%


00:43:53.850 --> 00:43:55.770 align:middle line:84%
Given the way everyone
else is bidding.

00:43:55.770 --> 00:44:00.610 align:middle line:84%
I could bid whatever I want,
but bidding b1 hat of v1

00:44:00.610 --> 00:44:02.270 align:middle line:90%
is the best possible bid for me.

00:44:02.270 --> 00:44:04.390 align:middle line:84%
It maximizes my
expected utility.

00:44:04.390 --> 00:44:07.610 align:middle line:90%


00:44:07.610 --> 00:44:14.010 align:middle line:84%
And a weird way you
want to think about this

00:44:14.010 --> 00:44:19.850 align:middle line:84%
is that tells me that
bidding b1 hat of v1

00:44:19.850 --> 00:44:22.250 align:middle line:84%
is better than any
other bid I could make.

00:44:22.250 --> 00:44:24.917 align:middle line:84%
But these other bids, as
you said, they're numbers.

00:44:24.917 --> 00:44:25.750 align:middle line:90%
They could be words.

00:44:25.750 --> 00:44:27.542 align:middle line:84%
We don't really know
what these things are.

00:44:27.542 --> 00:44:33.490 align:middle line:84%
But in particular, what this
tells us is that bidding--

00:44:33.490 --> 00:44:35.810 align:middle line:84%
and this is the
key observation--

00:44:35.810 --> 00:44:46.010 align:middle line:84%
bidding b1 hat of
v1 is weakly better

00:44:46.010 --> 00:44:52.270 align:middle line:84%
than bidding b1 hat of v1
prime for all v1 prime.

00:44:52.270 --> 00:44:55.450 align:middle line:90%


00:44:55.450 --> 00:44:57.430 align:middle line:84%
So in a sense, I haven't
done anything here,

00:44:57.430 --> 00:44:59.990 align:middle line:84%
but this actually turns out
to be the crucial observation.

00:44:59.990 --> 00:45:03.970 align:middle line:84%
So the point is,
bidding b1 hat of v1

00:45:03.970 --> 00:45:05.770 align:middle line:90%
is better than any other bid.

00:45:05.770 --> 00:45:08.850 align:middle line:84%
So in particular, what's
another bid I could make?

00:45:08.850 --> 00:45:10.650 align:middle line:84%
I could choose the
bid that I'm supposed

00:45:10.650 --> 00:45:13.210 align:middle line:84%
to make in this equilibrium
if my two valuation is

00:45:13.210 --> 00:45:16.430 align:middle line:84%
v1 prime. v1 prime is just
a different valuation.

00:45:16.430 --> 00:45:18.770 align:middle line:90%
I could do that.

00:45:18.770 --> 00:45:21.650 align:middle line:84%
But following my
equilibrium bidding strategy

00:45:21.650 --> 00:45:26.810 align:middle line:84%
must be better than this
alternative bid b1 of v prime.

00:45:26.810 --> 00:45:28.710 align:middle line:84%
And then the name
of the game here

00:45:28.710 --> 00:45:30.460 align:middle line:84%
is just to try to write
out mathematically

00:45:30.460 --> 00:45:34.620 align:middle line:84%
what this means in terms
of these functions here.

00:45:34.620 --> 00:45:36.820 align:middle line:84%
So it's very useful
to contemplate

00:45:36.820 --> 00:45:39.580 align:middle line:84%
this particular
alternative bid because we

00:45:39.580 --> 00:45:41.240 align:middle line:90%
can use this alternative bid.

00:45:41.240 --> 00:45:44.420 align:middle line:84%
We can express my utility from
this alternative bid in terms

00:45:44.420 --> 00:45:45.780 align:middle line:90%
of these functions here.

00:45:45.780 --> 00:45:47.480 align:middle line:90%
And that's really the key step.

00:45:47.480 --> 00:45:49.760 align:middle line:90%
So let's come back over here.

00:45:49.760 --> 00:46:06.660 align:middle line:90%


00:46:06.660 --> 00:46:09.260 align:middle line:84%
So I really want to emphasize
everything we're going to do now

00:46:09.260 --> 00:46:11.225 align:middle line:84%
is just following from
this simple observation.

00:46:11.225 --> 00:46:13.600 align:middle line:84%
So if there's any questions
on this, I think this is key.

00:46:13.600 --> 00:46:14.660 align:middle line:90%
Yes, thank you.

00:46:14.660 --> 00:46:17.940 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So does that imply
that for every item in the bid

00:46:17.940 --> 00:46:21.373 align:middle line:84%
set, there's some
valuation that maps to it?

00:46:21.373 --> 00:46:23.040 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: That's a
very subtle question.

00:46:23.040 --> 00:46:23.998 align:middle line:90%
It does not imply that.

00:46:23.998 --> 00:46:28.202 align:middle line:90%


00:46:28.202 --> 00:46:29.660 align:middle line:84%
So I think you're
basically asking,

00:46:29.660 --> 00:46:31.400 align:middle line:90%
are the bidding functions onto?

00:46:31.400 --> 00:46:33.740 align:middle line:90%
Are they subjective functions?

00:46:33.740 --> 00:46:36.540 align:middle line:84%
If they were onto, then these
statements would be equivalent.

00:46:36.540 --> 00:46:38.553 align:middle line:84%
In general, the bidding
function's not onto.

00:46:38.553 --> 00:46:40.220 align:middle line:84%
So it's still the
case that this implies

00:46:40.220 --> 00:46:41.760 align:middle line:90%
this but not the converse.

00:46:41.760 --> 00:46:44.580 align:middle line:84%
So we're not using
that assumption.

00:46:44.580 --> 00:46:46.420 align:middle line:84%
If that assumption is
violated, then we're

00:46:46.420 --> 00:46:47.700 align:middle line:84%
actually throwing
away information

00:46:47.700 --> 00:46:49.492 align:middle line:84%
because we're saying,
we know that this bit

00:46:49.492 --> 00:46:51.660 align:middle line:84%
is better than every
other bid, even bids

00:46:51.660 --> 00:46:53.760 align:middle line:84%
that no one ever
chooses in equilibrium.

00:46:53.760 --> 00:46:57.100 align:middle line:84%
But in particular, we know
this, so we're throwing away

00:46:57.100 --> 00:46:58.640 align:middle line:90%
information potentially.

00:46:58.640 --> 00:47:00.428 align:middle line:84%
We're not even
considering-- it's true

00:47:00.428 --> 00:47:02.720 align:middle line:84%
that this bid is better than
a bid that's never chosen,

00:47:02.720 --> 00:47:04.600 align:middle line:84%
but we don't even
need to use that fact.

00:47:04.600 --> 00:47:06.460 align:middle line:90%
So we're not requiring that.

00:47:06.460 --> 00:47:07.100 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:47:07.100 --> 00:47:11.580 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So why can b1
hat of v1 prime technically

00:47:11.580 --> 00:47:14.880 align:middle line:84%
just represent all other
possible bidding strategies?

00:47:14.880 --> 00:47:17.620 align:middle line:90%


00:47:17.620 --> 00:47:19.100 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: You said--

00:47:19.100 --> 00:47:20.000 align:middle line:90%
could you maybe--

00:47:20.000 --> 00:47:21.580 align:middle line:90%
I'm not sure if I quite follow.

00:47:21.580 --> 00:47:23.740 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Because there's
infinite other bids.

00:47:23.740 --> 00:47:24.698 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: You're right.

00:47:24.698 --> 00:47:28.660 align:middle line:84%
So I think the key here is I'm
just using this forward arrow.

00:47:28.660 --> 00:47:30.023 align:middle line:90%
I'm not saying that this--

00:47:30.023 --> 00:47:32.440 align:middle line:84%
that if this is true-- and
this relates to this question--

00:47:32.440 --> 00:47:33.660 align:middle line:84%
I'm not saying if
this is true, then

00:47:33.660 --> 00:47:34.907 align:middle line:90%
this must be in equilibrium.

00:47:34.907 --> 00:47:36.240 align:middle line:90%
I'm only going in one direction.

00:47:36.240 --> 00:47:38.780 align:middle line:84%
I'm saying, we know that
this is an equilibrium,

00:47:38.780 --> 00:47:40.780 align:middle line:84%
and an implication
of the fact that it's

00:47:40.780 --> 00:47:43.080 align:middle line:84%
in equilibrium, because
it's equilibrium,

00:47:43.080 --> 00:47:45.300 align:middle line:84%
I'm better off
bidding b hat of v1

00:47:45.300 --> 00:47:47.940 align:middle line:90%
than making any alternative bid.

00:47:47.940 --> 00:47:50.500 align:middle line:84%
So in particular, one
implication of that is I

00:47:50.500 --> 00:47:51.560 align:middle line:90%
don't want to bid 5.

00:47:51.560 --> 00:47:52.600 align:middle line:90%
I don't a bid 3.

00:47:52.600 --> 00:47:53.787 align:middle line:90%
I don't want to bid this.

00:47:53.787 --> 00:47:56.120 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we're not considering
all the other possible bids.

00:47:56.120 --> 00:47:58.180 align:middle line:90%
That's fine.

00:47:58.180 --> 00:48:00.040 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we're throwing
away information here,

00:48:00.040 --> 00:48:03.180 align:middle line:84%
but it's certainly an
implication of equilibrium

00:48:03.180 --> 00:48:04.158 align:middle line:90%
that this must hold.

00:48:04.158 --> 00:48:06.200 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: When you say
throwing away information,

00:48:06.200 --> 00:48:08.660 align:middle line:90%
what are you referring to?

00:48:08.660 --> 00:48:11.780 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: What I mean is the
definition of equilibrium

00:48:11.780 --> 00:48:13.820 align:middle line:84%
tells a lot of
things, and I'm only

00:48:13.820 --> 00:48:16.700 align:middle line:84%
using some logical
implications of the definition

00:48:16.700 --> 00:48:18.300 align:middle line:90%
of equilibrium.

00:48:18.300 --> 00:48:19.820 align:middle line:84%
So it's like I
have 10 equations,

00:48:19.820 --> 00:48:21.460 align:middle line:90%
and I'm only using nine of them.

00:48:21.460 --> 00:48:24.240 align:middle line:84%
And the 10th equation's true,
but I'm just not focusing on it.

00:48:24.240 --> 00:48:26.780 align:middle line:90%
I'm not using that fact.

00:48:26.780 --> 00:48:29.378 align:middle line:84%
And maybe this
all abstract stuff

00:48:29.378 --> 00:48:31.420 align:middle line:84%
makes more sense once we
go through the argument.

00:48:31.420 --> 00:48:35.645 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So you're saying like
bidding b1 hat v1 is optimal.

00:48:35.645 --> 00:48:38.020 align:middle line:84%
And there's other strategies
that we're not comparing to.

00:48:38.020 --> 00:48:39.900 align:middle line:90%
We're just comparing two.

00:48:39.900 --> 00:48:42.400 align:middle line:84%
The bidding of b1 hat but
with a different market.

00:48:42.400 --> 00:48:45.380 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Exactly, so the
fact that bidding b1 hat of v1

00:48:45.380 --> 00:48:47.240 align:middle line:90%
is optimal tells me a lot.

00:48:47.240 --> 00:48:48.940 align:middle line:84%
It tells me that
bidding b1 hat of v1

00:48:48.940 --> 00:48:52.020 align:middle line:84%
is better than any other
bid I could come up with.

00:48:52.020 --> 00:48:55.780 align:middle line:84%
But it's certainly better
than these special bids,

00:48:55.780 --> 00:48:57.520 align:middle line:84%
and I'm only going to
use that fact here.

00:48:57.520 --> 00:48:59.280 align:middle line:84%
Even though I know it's better
than some other bits too,

00:48:59.280 --> 00:49:00.280 align:middle line:90%
I'm not going to worry--

00:49:00.280 --> 00:49:02.300 align:middle line:84%
I don't even need
to use that fact.

00:49:02.300 --> 00:49:06.360 align:middle line:84%
OK, so now, as we
said, not equivalent

00:49:06.360 --> 00:49:09.303 align:middle line:84%
but an implication of
equilibrium is this observation.

00:49:09.303 --> 00:49:11.220 align:middle line:84%
And now we just want to
write this observation

00:49:11.220 --> 00:49:14.260 align:middle line:84%
down mathematically
using our notation here.

00:49:14.260 --> 00:49:17.600 align:middle line:84%
So what we want to say is,
what is my expected utility

00:49:17.600 --> 00:49:18.940 align:middle line:90%
if I bid this?

00:49:18.940 --> 00:49:20.800 align:middle line:84%
What is my expected
utility if I bid this?

00:49:20.800 --> 00:49:22.967 align:middle line:84%
And then we want to have
an inequality between them.

00:49:22.967 --> 00:49:25.070 align:middle line:90%
So let's take the first.

00:49:25.070 --> 00:49:32.410 align:middle line:84%
So what is my utility
from bidding b1 hat of v1?

00:49:32.410 --> 00:49:35.310 align:middle line:90%


00:49:35.310 --> 00:49:41.670 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's going to be
v1 times the probability

00:49:41.670 --> 00:49:51.930 align:middle line:84%
that I win minus my
expected payment.

00:49:51.930 --> 00:49:56.430 align:middle line:90%


00:49:56.430 --> 00:49:57.770 align:middle line:90%
This is what my utility is.

00:49:57.770 --> 00:49:59.870 align:middle line:84%
When I enter an auction,
I say, well, this

00:49:59.870 --> 00:50:01.550 align:middle line:90%
is my probability of winning.

00:50:01.550 --> 00:50:04.110 align:middle line:84%
Whenever I win, I get my
valuation for the good,

00:50:04.110 --> 00:50:05.350 align:middle line:90%
but then I have to pay.

00:50:05.350 --> 00:50:07.510 align:middle line:84%
Well, we'll just subtract
my expected payment

00:50:07.510 --> 00:50:10.310 align:middle line:84%
because we're assuming here
people are risk neutral.

00:50:10.310 --> 00:50:13.710 align:middle line:84%
So what is my probability of
winning if I bid b1 hat of v1

00:50:13.710 --> 00:50:15.010 align:middle line:90%
using our notation over there?

00:50:15.010 --> 00:50:22.070 align:middle line:90%


00:50:22.070 --> 00:50:25.350 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Just a few hat of 1.

00:50:25.350 --> 00:50:26.830 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Of v1, right?

00:50:26.830 --> 00:50:30.990 align:middle line:90%
So this is q1 hat of v1.

00:50:30.990 --> 00:50:33.666 align:middle line:84%
And then what is my
expected payment?

00:50:33.666 --> 00:50:34.618 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: It would--

00:50:34.618 --> 00:50:35.285 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Right.

00:50:35.285 --> 00:50:40.190 align:middle line:90%


00:50:40.190 --> 00:50:43.910 align:middle line:84%
Now the tricky part,
what if I instead bid--

00:50:43.910 --> 00:50:45.710 align:middle line:84%
I deviate, and
instead of bidding

00:50:45.710 --> 00:50:47.450 align:middle line:84%
what I'm supposed to
bid in equilibrium,

00:50:47.450 --> 00:50:48.450 align:middle line:90%
I make a different bid.

00:50:48.450 --> 00:50:55.470 align:middle line:84%
Specifically, I bid
v1 hat of v1 prime.

00:50:55.470 --> 00:50:58.810 align:middle line:84%
Well, the crucial point
is, what is my utility?

00:50:58.810 --> 00:51:01.410 align:middle line:84%
Well, first, I still
have a v1 here.

00:51:01.410 --> 00:51:02.770 align:middle line:90%
Let's not get confused.

00:51:02.770 --> 00:51:03.888 align:middle line:90%
My value is still v1.

00:51:03.888 --> 00:51:05.430 align:middle line:84%
The fact that I'm
bidding differently

00:51:05.430 --> 00:51:07.250 align:middle line:84%
doesn't change my
value to v1 prime.

00:51:07.250 --> 00:51:08.910 align:middle line:84%
My value is still
v1, so it's going

00:51:08.910 --> 00:51:12.470 align:middle line:84%
to be my value here, times,
again, my probability of winning

00:51:12.470 --> 00:51:16.190 align:middle line:90%
minus my expected payment.

00:51:16.190 --> 00:51:19.690 align:middle line:84%
What is the probability of
winning now going to be?

00:51:19.690 --> 00:51:21.510 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: b1 hat v1 prime.

00:51:21.510 --> 00:51:23.110 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Right,
so I just plug in--

00:51:23.110 --> 00:51:26.250 align:middle line:84%
if I look carefully
at this formula,

00:51:26.250 --> 00:51:30.568 align:middle line:84%
if I replace b1 hat of v1
with b1 hat of v1 prime,

00:51:30.568 --> 00:51:32.110 align:middle line:84%
well, then that's
just the definition

00:51:32.110 --> 00:51:35.310 align:middle line:84%
of this function
evaluated at v1 prime.

00:51:35.310 --> 00:51:45.910 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to get Q1
hat v1 prime plus T1 hat.

00:51:45.910 --> 00:51:46.810 align:middle line:90%
And this makes sense.

00:51:46.810 --> 00:51:48.060 align:middle line:90%
What's the definition of this?

00:51:48.060 --> 00:51:50.550 align:middle line:84%
This tells me if I
use bidding-- if I

00:51:50.550 --> 00:51:53.070 align:middle line:84%
use the bid b1 hat
of v1 prime, what

00:51:53.070 --> 00:51:56.270 align:middle line:90%
is my probability of winning?

00:51:56.270 --> 00:51:57.750 align:middle line:84%
And the crucial
thing going on here

00:51:57.750 --> 00:51:59.630 align:middle line:84%
is that my probability
of winning only

00:51:59.630 --> 00:52:01.090 align:middle line:90%
depends on how much I bid.

00:52:01.090 --> 00:52:02.930 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't depend on
my true valuation.

00:52:02.930 --> 00:52:04.290 align:middle line:90%
It just depends on what I bid.

00:52:04.290 --> 00:52:07.230 align:middle line:84%
So the v1 prime here is
really reflecting my bid, not

00:52:07.230 --> 00:52:09.710 align:middle line:90%
my true valuation.

00:52:09.710 --> 00:52:11.430 align:middle line:90%
So now what do we know?

00:52:11.430 --> 00:52:14.010 align:middle line:84%
Well, we know this is greater
than or equal to this.

00:52:14.010 --> 00:52:17.060 align:middle line:84%
And maybe I've interpreted
things so much,

00:52:17.060 --> 00:52:18.720 align:middle line:84%
it's nicer to
start a clean board

00:52:18.720 --> 00:52:23.000 align:middle line:84%
and just write down
our inequality.

00:52:23.000 --> 00:52:23.980 align:middle line:90%
So what do we know?

00:52:23.980 --> 00:52:34.040 align:middle line:90%


00:52:34.040 --> 00:52:41.280 align:middle line:84%
So what we know is that
for all v1, v1 prime--

00:52:41.280 --> 00:52:44.640 align:middle line:84%
remember, the interpretation
here is v1 is my true value.

00:52:44.640 --> 00:52:49.200 align:middle line:84%
v1 prime is the value that I
pretend to have when I bid.

00:52:49.200 --> 00:53:00.560 align:middle line:84%
Well, we must have v1 Q1 hat
of v1 T1 hat of v1 greater than

00:53:00.560 --> 00:53:01.300 align:middle line:90%
or equal to--

00:53:01.300 --> 00:53:10.960 align:middle line:90%


00:53:10.960 --> 00:53:13.140 align:middle line:84%
so I've just brought
that equation down here.

00:53:13.140 --> 00:53:19.280 align:middle line:90%


00:53:19.280 --> 00:53:22.480 align:middle line:84%
And maybe it's easier
if we say for all v1,

00:53:22.480 --> 00:53:27.360 align:middle line:84%
this holds for all v1 prime,
just a slightly different

00:53:27.360 --> 00:53:28.560 align:middle line:90%
perspective.

00:53:28.560 --> 00:53:34.120 align:middle line:90%


00:53:34.120 --> 00:53:38.320 align:middle line:90%
Well, what does this tell me?

00:53:38.320 --> 00:53:41.480 align:middle line:84%
Well, I have some function
on the right side that

00:53:41.480 --> 00:53:45.080 align:middle line:84%
depends on v1 prime,
but it's always smaller

00:53:45.080 --> 00:53:47.960 align:middle line:90%
than this left-hand side.

00:53:47.960 --> 00:53:50.320 align:middle line:84%
So in particular,
what that tells

00:53:50.320 --> 00:53:55.280 align:middle line:84%
me is that the value
of v1 prime that

00:53:55.280 --> 00:53:59.000 align:middle line:84%
maximizes this right-hand
side must be V1 itself.

00:53:59.000 --> 00:54:02.000 align:middle line:84%
So that tells me that V1,
let's use our arg max notation

00:54:02.000 --> 00:54:07.820 align:middle line:84%
is an element of the arg
max over v1 prime of v1.

00:54:07.820 --> 00:54:16.520 align:middle line:90%


00:54:16.520 --> 00:54:17.860 align:middle line:90%
In words, what is this saying?

00:54:17.860 --> 00:54:19.960 align:middle line:84%
It's saying if my
true value is v1,

00:54:19.960 --> 00:54:21.880 align:middle line:84%
I could bid as if
my valuation is

00:54:21.880 --> 00:54:25.080 align:middle line:84%
v1 prime for any other
value of v1 prime.

00:54:25.080 --> 00:54:28.200 align:middle line:84%
But because we know that
I'm playing an equilibrium,

00:54:28.200 --> 00:54:31.440 align:middle line:84%
the best thing for me to do
is to bid as if I am myself,

00:54:31.440 --> 00:54:34.680 align:middle line:84%
to bid as if my value
v1 prime is exactly v1,

00:54:34.680 --> 00:54:36.700 align:middle line:84%
and that's what this
is telling us here.

00:54:36.700 --> 00:54:40.280 align:middle line:90%


00:54:40.280 --> 00:54:43.360 align:middle line:84%
And now let's just compute
the first-order condition.

00:54:43.360 --> 00:54:47.503 align:middle line:84%
So if v1 is a maximizer
of this, then we

00:54:47.503 --> 00:54:49.920 align:middle line:84%
should be able to take the
derivative of this with respect

00:54:49.920 --> 00:54:53.700 align:middle line:84%
to v1 prime, and then plug
in v1 prime equals v1,

00:54:53.700 --> 00:54:54.620 align:middle line:90%
and we should get 0.

00:54:54.620 --> 00:54:56.053 align:middle line:90%
So let's do that.

00:54:56.053 --> 00:54:58.220 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to be a little
tricky with the primes.

00:54:58.220 --> 00:55:00.012 align:middle line:84%
You have to interpret
the primes carefully.

00:55:00.012 --> 00:55:05.800 align:middle line:84%
So this is v1 Q1 hat prime
v1 prime minus T1 hat

00:55:05.800 --> 00:55:07.680 align:middle line:90%
prime v1 prime.

00:55:07.680 --> 00:55:11.260 align:middle line:84%
Evaluated at v1 prime
equals v1, this should be 0.

00:55:11.260 --> 00:55:15.240 align:middle line:90%


00:55:15.240 --> 00:55:19.440 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, the primes, this prime
on the inside is just a symbol.

00:55:19.440 --> 00:55:21.400 align:middle line:84%
And this prime on the
outside is a derivative.

00:55:21.400 --> 00:55:24.400 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, that can be a
bit confusing here.

00:55:24.400 --> 00:55:26.420 align:middle line:84%
So you could call v1
prime z if you wanted.

00:55:26.420 --> 00:55:29.640 align:middle line:90%
It wouldn't make a difference.

00:55:29.640 --> 00:55:31.600 align:middle line:90%
And what do we get?

00:55:31.600 --> 00:55:35.920 align:middle line:90%
We get v1 Q1 prime v1.

00:55:35.920 --> 00:55:47.300 align:middle line:90%


00:55:47.300 --> 00:55:48.860 align:middle line:90%
Minus, sorry.

00:55:48.860 --> 00:55:52.015 align:middle line:90%


00:55:52.015 --> 00:55:54.640 align:middle line:84%
So this equals 0, or I could say
it implies that they're equal.

00:55:54.640 --> 00:56:00.600 align:middle line:90%


00:56:00.600 --> 00:56:02.640 align:middle line:90%
Well, so a lot of work.

00:56:02.640 --> 00:56:04.800 align:middle line:84%
But now we've narrowed
things down a lot.

00:56:04.800 --> 00:56:07.770 align:middle line:84%
I have a formula for the
derivative of this expected

00:56:07.770 --> 00:56:11.570 align:middle line:84%
payment that I make in terms
of my probability of getting

00:56:11.570 --> 00:56:13.330 align:middle line:90%
the good.

00:56:13.330 --> 00:56:17.650 align:middle line:84%
So now the trick is we're
just going to integrate this.

00:56:17.650 --> 00:56:21.490 align:middle line:84%
So if this equals
0, that tells me

00:56:21.490 --> 00:56:24.730 align:middle line:84%
that this derivative
equals this.

00:56:24.730 --> 00:56:27.035 align:middle line:84%
So now we can just integrate
this from v lower bar

00:56:27.035 --> 00:56:27.910 align:middle line:90%
and see what happens.

00:56:27.910 --> 00:56:30.250 align:middle line:84%
Actually, maybe I'll
just do it right here.

00:56:30.250 --> 00:56:32.530 align:middle line:90%
So what does this tell me?

00:56:32.530 --> 00:56:41.010 align:middle line:84%
It tells me that T1 hat
of V1 is equal to T1

00:56:41.010 --> 00:56:46.690 align:middle line:84%
hat of v lower bar plus an
integral from v lower bar

00:56:46.690 --> 00:56:50.590 align:middle line:90%
to v1 of x--

00:56:50.590 --> 00:56:56.510 align:middle line:90%
I'll change to x.

00:56:56.510 --> 00:57:01.610 align:middle line:90%


00:57:01.610 --> 00:57:03.390 align:middle line:84%
Again, when we
start integrating,

00:57:03.390 --> 00:57:05.150 align:middle line:90%
you have to be careful.

00:57:05.150 --> 00:57:08.323 align:middle line:84%
I've used a new dummy
variable here, x.

00:57:08.323 --> 00:57:09.490 align:middle line:90%
And there's a prime missing.

00:57:09.490 --> 00:57:23.010 align:middle line:90%


00:57:23.010 --> 00:57:27.590 align:middle line:84%
OK, so this is going to be
the key revenue formula.

00:57:27.590 --> 00:57:32.170 align:middle line:90%


00:57:32.170 --> 00:57:36.222 align:middle line:90%
Now, why is this so powerful?

00:57:36.222 --> 00:57:37.430 align:middle line:90%
I think we want to step back.

00:57:37.430 --> 00:57:40.013 align:middle line:84%
And at first, you might say, OK,
we just did a sea of algebra.

00:57:40.013 --> 00:57:42.210 align:middle line:90%
What's going on?

00:57:42.210 --> 00:57:44.450 align:middle line:90%
Well, what is this giving me?

00:57:44.450 --> 00:57:49.850 align:middle line:84%
It's giving me a formula for,
if I'm player 1 and my valuation

00:57:49.850 --> 00:57:53.330 align:middle line:84%
is v1, and we're
considering an equilibrium

00:57:53.330 --> 00:57:54.930 align:middle line:90%
of this generalized auction.

00:57:54.930 --> 00:57:58.330 align:middle line:84%
It's giving me a formula
for the expected payment

00:57:58.330 --> 00:57:59.930 align:middle line:90%
that I make in this auction.

00:57:59.930 --> 00:58:04.100 align:middle line:84%
But let's understand,
in general, you

00:58:04.100 --> 00:58:05.850 align:middle line:84%
might think that my
expected payment would

00:58:05.850 --> 00:58:07.290 align:middle line:90%
depend on a lot of things.

00:58:07.290 --> 00:58:09.207 align:middle line:84%
You might think it would
depend on the details

00:58:09.207 --> 00:58:11.490 align:middle line:84%
of the auction and all
these complicated things.

00:58:11.490 --> 00:58:14.410 align:middle line:84%
But if we look here,
what is it depending on?

00:58:14.410 --> 00:58:17.450 align:middle line:90%
It's depending on two things.

00:58:17.450 --> 00:58:19.670 align:middle line:84%
Can someone interpret
these two terms?

00:58:19.670 --> 00:58:23.410 align:middle line:84%
These are the only things that
appear on the right-hand side.

00:58:23.410 --> 00:58:26.650 align:middle line:90%
So what is this first term?

00:58:26.650 --> 00:58:30.610 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The base cost
of beginning the auction.

00:58:30.610 --> 00:58:33.810 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Yeah, so it's just the
expected payment that the lowest

00:58:33.810 --> 00:58:37.190 align:middle line:90%
possible valuation person pays.

00:58:37.190 --> 00:58:39.690 align:middle line:84%
That's the one thing, and I
think you could interpret it

00:58:39.690 --> 00:58:41.410 align:middle line:90%
maybe that way.

00:58:41.410 --> 00:58:44.270 align:middle line:90%
And then what is Q1 prime--

00:58:44.270 --> 00:58:46.990 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the prime, let's just
focus on the function Q1 hat.

00:58:46.990 --> 00:58:49.130 align:middle line:90%
What is this function Q1 hat?

00:58:49.130 --> 00:58:52.015 align:middle line:84%
Can we interpret--
what does it mean?

00:58:52.015 --> 00:58:52.890 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, maybe, I think.

00:58:52.890 --> 00:58:53.170 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:58:53.170 --> 00:58:54.753 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's the
marginal likelihood

00:58:54.753 --> 00:58:58.490 align:middle line:84%
of winning for each
infinitesimal change

00:58:58.490 --> 00:59:01.327 align:middle line:90%
in your valuation.

00:59:01.327 --> 00:59:03.660 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: I think the integral
is making you think about

00:59:03.660 --> 00:59:05.917 align:middle line:84%
infinitesimal stuff,
but really, it's-- oh,

00:59:05.917 --> 00:59:07.500 align:middle line:84%
you're taking the
derivative, exactly.

00:59:07.500 --> 00:59:08.220 align:middle line:90%
Sorry, yes.

00:59:08.220 --> 00:59:11.082 align:middle line:84%
So sorry, you said, yes, that's
the derivative of it, exactly.

00:59:11.082 --> 00:59:12.540 align:middle line:84%
And if we take the
derivative, it's

00:59:12.540 --> 00:59:15.370 align:middle line:84%
just my probability
of winning, exactly.

00:59:15.370 --> 00:59:17.120 align:middle line:84%
And this is the
infinitesimal, sorry, yes.

00:59:17.120 --> 00:59:19.420 align:middle line:90%
Exactly, I forgot, yeah.

00:59:19.420 --> 00:59:23.700 align:middle line:84%
OK, so what it tells us is
that a lot of the details

00:59:23.700 --> 00:59:25.680 align:middle line:90%
of the auction don't matter.

00:59:25.680 --> 00:59:29.280 align:middle line:84%
I mean, whether we're bidding
what we name our bids--

00:59:29.280 --> 00:59:31.920 align:middle line:84%
notice the sets B1 through
Bn don't even appear here.

00:59:31.920 --> 00:59:34.180 align:middle line:84%
All that matters
is the probability

00:59:34.180 --> 00:59:36.980 align:middle line:84%
that I win the good in
equilibrium and the amount

00:59:36.980 --> 00:59:39.860 align:middle line:84%
I pay if I'm the
lowest-value person.

00:59:39.860 --> 00:59:44.500 align:middle line:84%
So we immediately see that only
a few details of an auction

00:59:44.500 --> 00:59:48.380 align:middle line:84%
equilibrium pin down or uniquely
determine the expected payment

00:59:48.380 --> 00:59:49.660 align:middle line:90%
that everyone makes.

00:59:49.660 --> 00:59:52.720 align:middle line:90%
So maybe I'll come over here.

00:59:52.720 --> 01:00:12.220 align:middle line:90%


01:00:12.220 --> 01:00:14.220 align:middle line:90%
So what's the implication?

01:00:14.220 --> 01:00:19.720 align:middle line:90%


01:00:19.720 --> 01:00:30.420 align:middle line:84%
That the expected
payment by, say,

01:00:30.420 --> 01:00:43.660 align:middle line:84%
bidder 1 when the valuation
is v1 depends only on--

01:00:43.660 --> 01:00:51.620 align:middle line:84%
well, T1 hat v lower bar,
which is the expected payment

01:00:51.620 --> 01:01:01.740 align:middle line:90%
of the lowest valuation--

01:01:01.740 --> 01:01:03.340 align:middle line:84%
more precisely, the
expected payment

01:01:03.340 --> 01:01:06.500 align:middle line:84%
that bidder 1 makes when
her valuation is the lowest

01:01:06.500 --> 01:01:08.580 align:middle line:90%
possible valuation.

01:01:08.580 --> 01:01:12.500 align:middle line:84%
And we had the derivative
here, but if it

01:01:12.500 --> 01:01:14.980 align:middle line:84%
depends on the derivative
of Q1 hat, well,

01:01:14.980 --> 01:01:16.400 align:middle line:90%
it also depends on Q1 hat.

01:01:16.400 --> 01:01:21.420 align:middle line:84%
So it's a bit cleaner to
say just depends on Q1 hat--

01:01:21.420 --> 01:01:29.800 align:middle line:84%
this function Q1 hat, which
is the win probability.

01:01:29.800 --> 01:01:33.860 align:middle line:90%


01:01:33.860 --> 01:01:35.160 align:middle line:90%
And this is an entire function.

01:01:35.160 --> 01:01:38.040 align:middle line:84%
So it's the win probability
of every valuation.

01:01:38.040 --> 01:01:47.420 align:middle line:90%


01:01:47.420 --> 01:01:50.780 align:middle line:84%
But the point is, in a lot
of different equilibria

01:01:50.780 --> 01:01:53.340 align:middle line:84%
of different auction
formats, these two values

01:01:53.340 --> 01:01:55.850 align:middle line:84%
take very-- these take
very special values.

01:01:55.850 --> 01:01:59.110 align:middle line:84%
So in many equilibria, in
many auctions that we've

01:01:59.110 --> 01:02:01.870 align:middle line:90%
looked at so far, what is--

01:02:01.870 --> 01:02:03.170 align:middle line:90%
so I'll just say often.

01:02:03.170 --> 01:02:06.270 align:middle line:90%


01:02:06.270 --> 01:02:14.350 align:middle line:84%
Well, intuitively, what do
you think T1 hat of v1 lower

01:02:14.350 --> 01:02:16.570 align:middle line:90%
bar is often going to be?

01:02:16.570 --> 01:02:17.150 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: 0.

01:02:17.150 --> 01:02:19.550 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: 0 because,
in most auctions,

01:02:19.550 --> 01:02:22.470 align:middle line:84%
if you have the lowest possible
valuation for the item,

01:02:22.470 --> 01:02:25.755 align:middle line:84%
in most equilibria, well,
you're never going to win.

01:02:25.755 --> 01:02:27.130 align:middle line:84%
And if you're
never going to win,

01:02:27.130 --> 01:02:28.590 align:middle line:90%
you don't want to pay anything.

01:02:28.590 --> 01:02:32.750 align:middle line:84%
So often, auction equilibria
have this property.

01:02:32.750 --> 01:02:36.150 align:middle line:84%
And now maybe this
is a more subtle one.

01:02:36.150 --> 01:02:38.910 align:middle line:90%
What about Q1 hat?

01:02:38.910 --> 01:02:42.190 align:middle line:90%
Maybe I'll write Q1 hat of v1?

01:02:42.190 --> 01:02:44.170 align:middle line:84%
Well, in a lot of
auction equilibria

01:02:44.170 --> 01:02:48.670 align:middle line:84%
we've looked at, which
bidder wins the item?

01:02:48.670 --> 01:02:49.690 align:middle line:90%
It's the highest bidder.

01:02:49.690 --> 01:02:51.470 align:middle line:84%
But another way of
saying it is it's

01:02:51.470 --> 01:02:53.220 align:middle line:84%
the bidder who bids
the most, that's true.

01:02:53.220 --> 01:02:56.710 align:middle line:84%
But it's also the bidder whose
valuation is the highest.

01:02:56.710 --> 01:02:58.930 align:middle line:84%
So often, when
auctions work well,

01:02:58.930 --> 01:03:01.870 align:middle line:84%
we might think that an
outcome of the auction

01:03:01.870 --> 01:03:05.270 align:middle line:84%
is that the bidder who values
the item most gets the item.

01:03:05.270 --> 01:03:08.350 align:middle line:84%
But if the bidder who values
the item most gets the item,

01:03:08.350 --> 01:03:15.230 align:middle line:84%
then what is Q hat
1 of v1 going to be?

01:03:15.230 --> 01:03:16.590 align:middle line:90%
Well-- Yeah.

01:03:16.590 --> 01:03:19.150 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Or as an expectation--

01:03:19.150 --> 01:03:20.530 align:middle line:90%
it will either be 0 or 1.

01:03:20.530 --> 01:03:22.190 align:middle line:84%
So it's a kind of average
of those probabilities.

01:03:22.190 --> 01:03:23.530 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Its an
expectation between 0 and 1,

01:03:23.530 --> 01:03:25.613 align:middle line:84%
so it's going to be a
probability between 0 and 1.

01:03:25.613 --> 01:03:27.470 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's true.

01:03:27.470 --> 01:03:28.650 align:middle line:90%
Can we say more about it?

01:03:28.650 --> 01:03:30.790 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:03:30.790 --> 01:03:32.990 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Maybe, but if
there are n other players,

01:03:32.990 --> 01:03:35.395 align:middle line:84%
then would be v1 to
the power of n minus 1.

01:03:35.395 --> 01:03:36.770 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: In the
uniform example,

01:03:36.770 --> 01:03:38.010 align:middle line:90%
that's exactly what it would be.

01:03:38.010 --> 01:03:39.510 align:middle line:84%
But more generally,
maybe let's just

01:03:39.510 --> 01:03:41.790 align:middle line:84%
write it would be the
probability that everyone else's

01:03:41.790 --> 01:03:43.517 align:middle line:84%
value is below v1
because if it goes

01:03:43.517 --> 01:03:45.350 align:middle line:84%
to the bidder with the
highest value, what's

01:03:45.350 --> 01:03:46.990 align:middle line:84%
the probability that
bidder 1 gets it

01:03:46.990 --> 01:03:48.090 align:middle line:90%
when the valuation is v1?

01:03:48.090 --> 01:03:49.715 align:middle line:84%
It's the probability
that everyone else

01:03:49.715 --> 01:03:50.650 align:middle line:90%
is value is below v1.

01:03:50.650 --> 01:03:57.750 align:middle line:84%
So it's the probability that
the max of j not equal to 1 vj

01:03:57.750 --> 01:04:00.230 align:middle line:90%
is less than or equal to v1.

01:04:00.230 --> 01:04:02.870 align:middle line:84%
And we actually have
notation for this.

01:04:02.870 --> 01:04:06.050 align:middle line:84%
In the symmetric case,
what is the notation?

01:04:06.050 --> 01:04:06.803 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:04:06.803 --> 01:04:07.470 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Big G.

01:04:07.470 --> 01:04:08.530 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Big G, right?

01:04:08.530 --> 01:04:10.740 align:middle line:90%
So this is also--

01:04:10.740 --> 01:04:13.830 align:middle line:90%
I'll write up here, big G of v1.

01:04:13.830 --> 01:04:17.590 align:middle line:90%


01:04:17.590 --> 01:04:20.070 align:middle line:90%
So we often see that this holds.

01:04:20.070 --> 01:04:24.310 align:middle line:84%
"Often" is in quotes, but
what we see is that these tend

01:04:24.310 --> 01:04:24.810 align:middle line:90%
to happen.

01:04:24.810 --> 01:04:27.910 align:middle line:84%
And if that happens, let's
plug into a formula over here.

01:04:27.910 --> 01:04:42.750 align:middle line:84%
So under these conditions,
what does T1 hat of v1 give us?

01:04:42.750 --> 01:04:48.050 align:middle line:84%
Well, what is this first
term under those conditions?

01:04:48.050 --> 01:04:48.550 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: 0.

01:04:48.550 --> 01:04:50.870 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: 0, OK, so
we can forget that.

01:04:50.870 --> 01:04:53.690 align:middle line:90%
Q1 hat 1 of x is what?

01:04:53.690 --> 01:04:56.950 align:middle line:90%


01:04:56.950 --> 01:04:59.470 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Small g of x.

01:04:59.470 --> 01:05:01.227 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Right,
so Q1 hat is big G.

01:05:01.227 --> 01:05:02.810 align:middle line:84%
And then, when we
take the derivative,

01:05:02.810 --> 01:05:09.310 align:middle line:84%
we get small G. So what we get
is the integral from v lower bar

01:05:09.310 --> 01:05:15.070 align:middle line:90%
to v1 of x g of x dx.

01:05:15.070 --> 01:05:17.550 align:middle line:90%
Does this look familiar?

01:05:17.550 --> 01:05:19.750 align:middle line:84%
This is exactly
the formula we had.

01:05:19.750 --> 01:05:21.850 align:middle line:84%
So now we understand
where it comes from.

01:05:21.850 --> 01:05:24.830 align:middle line:84%
So this is not a special
property of first-price auctions

01:05:24.830 --> 01:05:26.090 align:middle line:90%
or second-price auctions.

01:05:26.090 --> 01:05:28.790 align:middle line:84%
Now we understand exactly
when this is going to hold.

01:05:28.790 --> 01:05:31.870 align:middle line:84%
This is going to hold in any
equilibrium of any auction that

01:05:31.870 --> 01:05:34.230 align:middle line:90%
satisfies these two properties.

01:05:34.230 --> 01:05:39.630 align:middle line:84%
Whenever my value is the lowest
possible value, I pay nothing.

01:05:39.630 --> 01:05:41.230 align:middle line:90%
That's the condition here.

01:05:41.230 --> 01:05:43.790 align:middle line:84%
And if the good is
always allocated

01:05:43.790 --> 01:05:46.230 align:middle line:84%
to the bidder with the
highest valuation--

01:05:46.230 --> 01:05:48.272 align:middle line:84%
now, how that happens
is going to depend

01:05:48.272 --> 01:05:50.480 align:middle line:84%
on the details of the
equilibrium, what the bids are.

01:05:50.480 --> 01:05:52.753 align:middle line:84%
But as long as the
auction has that property,

01:05:52.753 --> 01:05:54.920 align:middle line:84%
then the expected payments
are going to be the same.

01:05:54.920 --> 01:05:57.600 align:middle line:84%
They're exactly going
to take this form.

01:05:57.600 --> 01:05:59.540 align:middle line:84%
So indeed, it wasn't
a coincidence.

01:05:59.540 --> 01:06:01.960 align:middle line:84%
It wasn't just something about
the first- and second-price

01:06:01.960 --> 01:06:02.660 align:middle line:90%
auction.

01:06:02.660 --> 01:06:03.980 align:middle line:90%
This is a very general fact.

01:06:03.980 --> 01:06:07.080 align:middle line:84%
So I think it's
good to understand--

01:06:07.080 --> 01:06:08.280 align:middle line:90%
I think sometimes when--

01:06:08.280 --> 01:06:09.760 align:middle line:84%
I should say exactly
how it's done

01:06:09.760 --> 01:06:11.480 align:middle line:84%
in the notes-- revenue
equivalents can be

01:06:11.480 --> 01:06:13.317 align:middle line:90%
stated in two different ways.

01:06:13.317 --> 01:06:15.400 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes people say only
revenue equivalence only

01:06:15.400 --> 01:06:16.692 align:middle line:90%
holds under certain conditions.

01:06:16.692 --> 01:06:17.640 align:middle line:90%
Let's understand.

01:06:17.640 --> 01:06:19.280 align:middle line:90%
This formula always holds--

01:06:19.280 --> 01:06:20.580 align:middle line:90%
or always.

01:06:20.580 --> 01:06:22.760 align:middle line:84%
This formula holds
quite generally,

01:06:22.760 --> 01:06:25.400 align:middle line:84%
but it reduces to
this special form

01:06:25.400 --> 01:06:26.980 align:middle line:90%
under the additional conditions.

01:06:26.980 --> 01:06:29.160 align:middle line:84%
So you can still apply
this formula to an auction

01:06:29.160 --> 01:06:31.120 align:middle line:84%
where the good isn't
always allocated

01:06:31.120 --> 01:06:33.300 align:middle line:84%
to the person with
the highest valuation

01:06:33.300 --> 01:06:36.220 align:middle line:84%
or where the lowest-valuation
person pays a positive amount.

01:06:36.220 --> 01:06:39.240 align:middle line:84%
This formula still holds,
but it reduces and simplifies

01:06:39.240 --> 01:06:40.560 align:middle line:90%
under those further conditions.

01:06:40.560 --> 01:06:41.740 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

01:06:41.740 --> 01:06:43.240 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Could you
revisit the step

01:06:43.240 --> 01:06:45.400 align:middle line:84%
of how we went through
taking the derivative

01:06:45.400 --> 01:06:47.217 align:middle line:90%
of our v1 equation?

01:06:47.217 --> 01:06:47.800 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yes.

01:06:47.800 --> 01:06:49.717 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: --and getting
to the revenue format?

01:06:49.717 --> 01:06:52.480 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Yes, so
from here to there?

01:06:52.480 --> 01:06:56.040 align:middle line:84%
OK, so you're good
up to this point?

01:06:56.040 --> 01:06:58.020 align:middle line:90%
OK, so we're at this point.

01:06:58.020 --> 01:07:00.860 align:middle line:84%
And it's a little spread
out, but this is 0 over here.

01:07:00.860 --> 01:07:10.680 align:middle line:84%
So the 0 over here tells me
that v1 times q1 hat prime of v1

01:07:10.680 --> 01:07:13.480 align:middle line:90%
equals T1 hat prime of v1.

01:07:13.480 --> 01:07:16.440 align:middle line:84%
And now, yeah, I think the way
I changed variables is tricky.

01:07:16.440 --> 01:07:17.960 align:middle line:84%
I think the best
thing to do would

01:07:17.960 --> 01:07:21.960 align:middle line:84%
be wherever you see a
v1, let's just put an x.

01:07:21.960 --> 01:07:26.960 align:middle line:84%
So this tells me x q1 prime
of x equals T1 prime of x.

01:07:26.960 --> 01:07:28.960 align:middle line:84%
Now I'm going to
integrate both sides

01:07:28.960 --> 01:07:32.960 align:middle line:84%
from x equals v lower
bar to x equals v1.

01:07:32.960 --> 01:07:38.760 align:middle line:84%
So when I integrate
the derivative of T1

01:07:38.760 --> 01:07:44.480 align:middle line:84%
from v lower bar to v1, I
just get t hat of v1 minus t

01:07:44.480 --> 01:07:45.560 align:middle line:90%
hat of v lower bar.

01:07:45.560 --> 01:07:48.240 align:middle line:84%
So you could think of that
coming to the left-hand side.

01:07:48.240 --> 01:07:52.568 align:middle line:84%
And then, when I integrate
this, well, I exactly get this.

01:07:52.568 --> 01:07:53.860 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK, that makes sense.

01:07:53.860 --> 01:07:54.120 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Clear?

01:07:54.120 --> 01:07:54.460 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:07:54.460 --> 01:07:55.210 align:middle line:90%
Thanks for asking.

01:07:55.210 --> 01:07:56.880 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

01:07:56.880 --> 01:07:58.600 align:middle line:90%
So yeah, it was tricky.

01:07:58.600 --> 01:08:01.860 align:middle line:84%
I used v1 here, but then v1
becomes my dummy variable x.

01:08:01.860 --> 01:08:03.140 align:middle line:90%
And then I integrate up to v1.

01:08:03.140 --> 01:08:06.360 align:middle line:90%


01:08:06.360 --> 01:08:08.360 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

01:08:08.360 --> 01:08:10.440 align:middle line:84%
So you might say, OK,
this is a nice theory,

01:08:10.440 --> 01:08:12.322 align:middle line:90%
but how can we ever use this?

01:08:12.322 --> 01:08:14.280 align:middle line:84%
Well, it turns out this
can be a convenient way

01:08:14.280 --> 01:08:17.279 align:middle line:84%
to solve for
equilibria in auctions.

01:08:17.279 --> 01:08:19.840 align:middle line:90%
So let's go over a mini--

01:08:19.840 --> 01:08:21.700 align:middle line:84%
an application of
revenue equivalence.

01:08:21.700 --> 01:08:24.880 align:middle line:90%


01:08:24.880 --> 01:08:27.120 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll call it RET, an
application of the Revenue

01:08:27.120 --> 01:08:30.000 align:middle line:90%
Equivalence Theorem.

01:08:30.000 --> 01:08:31.380 align:middle line:90%
Let's look at all-pay auction.

01:08:31.380 --> 01:08:37.840 align:middle line:84%
So let's do our simple case
where we have just two bidders,

01:08:37.840 --> 01:08:40.920 align:middle line:90%
v1 and v2.

01:08:40.920 --> 01:08:43.189 align:middle line:84%
And they're in 0, 1,
and they're uniform.

01:08:43.189 --> 01:08:50.330 align:middle line:90%


01:08:50.330 --> 01:08:52.675 align:middle line:84%
And now, instead of considering
the first-price auction

01:08:52.675 --> 01:08:55.050 align:middle line:84%
or the second-price auction,
let's consider what's called

01:08:55.050 --> 01:08:58.090 align:middle line:90%
an All-Pay Auction, APA.

01:08:58.090 --> 01:09:01.010 align:middle line:84%
So in an all-pay auction,
the allocation rule

01:09:01.010 --> 01:09:03.270 align:middle line:84%
is the same as the first-
and second-price auction.

01:09:03.270 --> 01:09:06.689 align:middle line:84%
We allocate the good
to the highest bidder.

01:09:06.689 --> 01:09:10.510 align:middle line:84%
So we allocate to
highest bidder.

01:09:10.510 --> 01:09:16.210 align:middle line:90%


01:09:16.210 --> 01:09:19.350 align:middle line:84%
But everyone has to pay their
bids, whether they win or lose.

01:09:19.350 --> 01:09:28.457 align:middle line:90%


01:09:28.457 --> 01:09:30.290 align:middle line:84%
So I think it should
be quite intuitive here

01:09:30.290 --> 01:09:32.649 align:middle line:84%
that you don't want to bid
very much in this auction

01:09:32.649 --> 01:09:35.609 align:middle line:84%
because you know you have to pay
your bid whether or not you win.

01:09:35.609 --> 01:09:40.850 align:middle line:84%
So intuitively,
people will bid less.

01:09:40.850 --> 01:09:43.390 align:middle line:90%
But we might guess--

01:09:43.390 --> 01:09:46.410 align:middle line:90%


01:09:46.410 --> 01:09:53.323 align:middle line:84%
so let's guess that there's
a symmetric equilibrium

01:09:53.323 --> 01:09:53.990 align:middle line:90%
of this auction.

01:09:53.990 --> 01:09:57.050 align:middle line:84%
Or let's look for a symmetric
equilibrium of this auction.

01:09:57.050 --> 01:09:58.590 align:middle line:90%
So a symmetric equilibrium.

01:09:58.590 --> 01:10:04.930 align:middle line:90%


01:10:04.930 --> 01:10:06.770 align:middle line:84%
Remember, a
symmetric equilibrium

01:10:06.770 --> 01:10:09.510 align:middle line:84%
means both players are using
the same bidding function.

01:10:09.510 --> 01:10:11.490 align:middle line:84%
So a symmetric
equilibrium means we're

01:10:11.490 --> 01:10:20.250 align:middle line:84%
looking for a function beta from
0, 1 to maybe 0 to infinity.

01:10:20.250 --> 01:10:22.450 align:middle line:84%
So we're looking for a
symmetric equilibrium

01:10:22.450 --> 01:10:25.410 align:middle line:84%
where both players follow the
bidding strategy beta that

01:10:25.410 --> 01:10:27.290 align:middle line:84%
says, if my valuation
is this, I'm going

01:10:27.290 --> 01:10:29.690 align:middle line:90%
to bid beta of my valuation.

01:10:29.690 --> 01:10:32.610 align:middle line:84%
So this is what we're
trying to solve for.

01:10:32.610 --> 01:10:33.870 align:middle line:90%
I'll go over here.

01:10:33.870 --> 01:10:47.770 align:middle line:90%


01:10:47.770 --> 01:10:50.138 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to guess
a symmetric equilibrium,

01:10:50.138 --> 01:10:51.430 align:middle line:90%
but with a few more properties.

01:10:51.430 --> 01:10:53.805 align:middle line:84%
I think we're going to guess
that there's an equilibrium,

01:10:53.805 --> 01:10:58.470 align:middle line:84%
and also, beta of 0 equals 0,
and beta is strictly increasing.

01:10:58.470 --> 01:11:03.370 align:middle line:90%


01:11:03.370 --> 01:11:06.450 align:middle line:84%
These seem pretty
reasonable guesses.

01:11:06.450 --> 01:11:09.150 align:middle line:84%
So we think, because the
environment's symmetric,

01:11:09.150 --> 01:11:11.410 align:middle line:84%
maybe people use symmetric
bidding functions.

01:11:11.410 --> 01:11:14.330 align:middle line:84%
It seems to make sense that
if your value is higher,

01:11:14.330 --> 01:11:15.450 align:middle line:90%
you should bid more.

01:11:15.450 --> 01:11:16.730 align:middle line:90%
That makes sense.

01:11:16.730 --> 01:11:20.070 align:middle line:84%
And why do we expect
beta of 0 to be 0?

01:11:20.070 --> 01:11:23.370 align:middle line:90%


01:11:23.370 --> 01:11:23.890 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:11:23.890 --> 01:11:26.090 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You wouldn't want
to bid anything positive

01:11:26.090 --> 01:11:28.022 align:middle line:84%
if the item isn't
worth anything.

01:11:28.022 --> 01:11:30.230 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Especially because
it's an all-pay auction.

01:11:30.230 --> 01:11:31.750 align:middle line:84%
I mean, that's always true,
but in all-pay auction,

01:11:31.750 --> 01:11:33.810 align:middle line:84%
if you bid something
positive, what happens?

01:11:33.810 --> 01:11:34.630 align:middle line:90%
You have to pay.

01:11:34.630 --> 01:11:35.830 align:middle line:84%
So you're paying
a positive amount.

01:11:35.830 --> 01:11:37.950 align:middle line:84%
And even if you get the
good, you don't value it.

01:11:37.950 --> 01:11:40.650 align:middle line:84%
So these seem like
pretty safe guesses.

01:11:40.650 --> 01:11:43.850 align:middle line:84%
But let's say there is an
equilibrium with this property.

01:11:43.850 --> 01:11:46.850 align:middle line:84%
Will our revenue equivalent
assumptions be satisfied?

01:11:46.850 --> 01:11:49.290 align:middle line:84%
So let's check if there's an
equilibrium of this property,

01:11:49.290 --> 01:12:01.750 align:middle line:84%
what is the expected payment by
someone whose valuation is 0?

01:12:01.750 --> 01:12:03.450 align:middle line:90%
What's it going to be?

01:12:03.450 --> 01:12:04.790 align:middle line:90%
Just 0.

01:12:04.790 --> 01:12:07.350 align:middle line:84%
If there's an equilibrium,
where, if my value is 0,

01:12:07.350 --> 01:12:11.090 align:middle line:84%
I bid nothing, well, then
I'm going to pay nothing.

01:12:11.090 --> 01:12:13.250 align:middle line:90%
It's going to be 0.

01:12:13.250 --> 01:12:17.690 align:middle line:84%
And then if we're both using the
same strictly increasing bidding

01:12:17.690 --> 01:12:20.810 align:middle line:84%
function, well, which
bidder is the good going

01:12:20.810 --> 01:12:21.830 align:middle line:90%
to be allocated to?

01:12:21.830 --> 01:12:27.350 align:middle line:90%


01:12:27.350 --> 01:12:27.850 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:12:27.850 --> 01:12:29.150 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Whoever has
a greater amount here.

01:12:29.150 --> 01:12:30.610 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: It's going to go to
whichever bidder has the highest

01:12:30.610 --> 01:12:32.902 align:middle line:84%
value because it's going to
go to whichever bidder bids

01:12:32.902 --> 01:12:35.380 align:middle line:84%
the most, but we're both using
the same increasing bidding

01:12:35.380 --> 01:12:36.160 align:middle line:90%
strategy.

01:12:36.160 --> 01:12:37.120 align:middle line:90%
So who's going to bid the most?

01:12:37.120 --> 01:12:38.860 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be the person
who has the higher value.

01:12:38.860 --> 01:12:40.400 align:middle line:90%
So it's going to be allocated--

01:12:40.400 --> 01:12:54.260 align:middle line:90%


01:12:54.260 --> 01:12:57.700 align:middle line:84%
So the two conditions we needed
to get the revenue formula

01:12:57.700 --> 01:13:00.020 align:middle line:90%
to simplify are satisfied.

01:13:00.020 --> 01:13:02.300 align:middle line:84%
And that means we
immediately know

01:13:02.300 --> 01:13:04.740 align:middle line:84%
what the expected payment
of each bidder has to be.

01:13:04.740 --> 01:13:06.540 align:middle line:90%
So what does that tell us?

01:13:06.540 --> 01:13:08.840 align:middle line:84%
Well, what is-- let's
just focus on bidder 1.

01:13:08.840 --> 01:13:10.540 align:middle line:84%
It'll be the same
for everyone else.

01:13:10.540 --> 01:13:14.637 align:middle line:90%
What is T1 hat of v1 then?

01:13:14.637 --> 01:13:15.720 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's go through it.

01:13:15.720 --> 01:13:18.140 align:middle line:90%
It's the integral.

01:13:18.140 --> 01:13:19.680 align:middle line:90%
Well, what is v lower bar?

01:13:19.680 --> 01:13:27.100 align:middle line:84%
It's just 0, 0 to
v1 of x g of x dx.

01:13:27.100 --> 01:13:28.280 align:middle line:90%
What is g of x here?

01:13:28.280 --> 01:13:36.668 align:middle line:90%


01:13:36.668 --> 01:13:37.383 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: 1.

01:13:37.383 --> 01:13:38.300 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: It's just 1.

01:13:38.300 --> 01:13:41.260 align:middle line:84%
Remember g equals f if
there's two bidders.

01:13:41.260 --> 01:13:43.860 align:middle line:84%
And if they're
uniform, g is just 1.

01:13:43.860 --> 01:13:45.900 align:middle line:90%
So why?

01:13:45.900 --> 01:13:49.940 align:middle line:84%
G is the density of the highest
valuation among my opponents.

01:13:49.940 --> 01:13:51.040 align:middle line:90%
There's only one of them.

01:13:51.040 --> 01:13:52.800 align:middle line:84%
So it's just the density of
the uniform distribution,

01:13:52.800 --> 01:13:53.580 align:middle line:90%
which is 1.

01:13:53.580 --> 01:13:54.560 align:middle line:90%
So what do we get?

01:13:54.560 --> 01:13:57.140 align:middle line:90%


01:13:57.140 --> 01:14:01.540 align:middle line:84%
Same formula that we
always got before--

01:14:01.540 --> 01:14:03.180 align:middle line:90%
v1 squared over 2.

01:14:03.180 --> 01:14:04.980 align:middle line:84%
Notice, this is
exactly what we got way

01:14:04.980 --> 01:14:06.240 align:middle line:90%
at the beginning of class.

01:14:06.240 --> 01:14:08.940 align:middle line:84%
So in general, this
is always the formula

01:14:08.940 --> 01:14:11.220 align:middle line:84%
for the expected payment
by a bidder with valuation

01:14:11.220 --> 01:14:16.000 align:middle line:84%
v1 in our simple two-bidder
uniform distribution case

01:14:16.000 --> 01:14:17.900 align:middle line:84%
as long as our
assumptions are satisfied.

01:14:17.900 --> 01:14:19.360 align:middle line:90%
But now I claim that we're done.

01:14:19.360 --> 01:14:22.400 align:middle line:84%
What must the
bidding function be?

01:14:22.400 --> 01:14:27.660 align:middle line:90%


01:14:27.660 --> 01:14:30.020 align:middle line:84%
So let's step back and
see what we've done.

01:14:30.020 --> 01:14:33.100 align:middle line:84%
We've guessed that this is
the bidding function beta.

01:14:33.100 --> 01:14:36.060 align:middle line:84%
We've said that if this is the
bidding function beta, then

01:14:36.060 --> 01:14:39.860 align:middle line:84%
the expected payment by bidder
1 when the valuation is v1

01:14:39.860 --> 01:14:42.260 align:middle line:84%
must exactly be
v1 squared over 2.

01:14:42.260 --> 01:14:44.740 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:14:44.740 --> 01:14:46.243 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Is it 1/2?

01:14:46.243 --> 01:14:47.160 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Even easier.

01:14:47.160 --> 01:14:50.540 align:middle line:90%
It's an all-pay auction.

01:14:50.540 --> 01:14:52.640 align:middle line:84%
So another way of writing
the expected payment--

01:14:52.640 --> 01:14:58.100 align:middle line:90%


01:14:58.100 --> 01:14:59.820 align:middle line:90%
beta v1.

01:14:59.820 --> 01:15:02.220 align:middle line:84%
If it's an all-pay auction,
my expected payment

01:15:02.220 --> 01:15:04.780 align:middle line:84%
is my bid because I
always pay my bid.

01:15:04.780 --> 01:15:07.580 align:middle line:84%
So of course, that's
my expected payment.

01:15:07.580 --> 01:15:09.080 align:middle line:90%
If I bid 2, I always pay 2.

01:15:09.080 --> 01:15:10.480 align:middle line:90%
If I bid 3, I always pay 3.

01:15:10.480 --> 01:15:11.680 align:middle line:90%
That's my expected payment.

01:15:11.680 --> 01:15:14.980 align:middle line:90%


01:15:14.980 --> 01:15:16.000 align:middle line:90%
So what's the answer?

01:15:16.000 --> 01:15:18.855 align:middle line:90%


01:15:18.855 --> 01:15:19.980 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: j squared over 2.

01:15:19.980 --> 01:15:21.480 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: That's
it, and we're done.

01:15:21.480 --> 01:15:23.220 align:middle line:90%
So we've now found--

01:15:23.220 --> 01:15:25.240 align:middle line:84%
I mean, technically
we should verify this.

01:15:25.240 --> 01:15:28.000 align:middle line:84%
We've only said, this is
a necessary condition,

01:15:28.000 --> 01:15:28.890 align:middle line:90%
but it is true.

01:15:28.890 --> 01:15:32.470 align:middle line:90%
So you can check if it's right.

01:15:32.470 --> 01:15:37.790 align:middle line:90%
Actually, it's v. We're done.

01:15:37.790 --> 01:15:41.270 align:middle line:84%
So revenue equivalence
can be a convenient way

01:15:41.270 --> 01:15:43.850 align:middle line:84%
of actually solving
for an equilibrium

01:15:43.850 --> 01:15:45.350 align:middle line:84%
because revenue
equivalence gives us

01:15:45.350 --> 01:15:47.550 align:middle line:84%
a very simple formula
for the expected payment

01:15:47.550 --> 01:15:49.470 align:middle line:90%
that each player makes.

01:15:49.470 --> 01:15:51.150 align:middle line:84%
This is from
revenue equivalence.

01:15:51.150 --> 01:15:53.230 align:middle line:84%
The auction format
gives us a relationship

01:15:53.230 --> 01:15:55.630 align:middle line:84%
between the expected
payment and the bid.

01:15:55.630 --> 01:15:57.970 align:middle line:84%
In the all-pay auction, it's
a very simple relationship.

01:15:57.970 --> 01:16:00.510 align:middle line:84%
The expected payment I
make is exactly my bid.

01:16:00.510 --> 01:16:03.210 align:middle line:84%
But now if we have two
different formulas,

01:16:03.210 --> 01:16:05.950 align:middle line:84%
two different expressions
for T1 hat of v1,

01:16:05.950 --> 01:16:08.230 align:middle line:90%
they must both be equal.

01:16:08.230 --> 01:16:09.170 align:middle line:90%
And now we have this.

01:16:09.170 --> 01:16:12.830 align:middle line:90%


01:16:12.830 --> 01:16:14.430 align:middle line:84%
Maybe just one
more example to see

01:16:14.430 --> 01:16:21.710 align:middle line:84%
how we could derive
the first-price auction

01:16:21.710 --> 01:16:23.090 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium using this.

01:16:23.090 --> 01:16:30.407 align:middle line:90%


01:16:30.407 --> 01:16:32.990 align:middle line:84%
Basically, we're going to do the
reverse of what we did before

01:16:32.990 --> 01:16:35.070 align:middle line:90%
at the beginning of class.

01:16:35.070 --> 01:16:37.130 align:middle line:84%
Let's consider a
first-price auction, again,

01:16:37.130 --> 01:16:39.870 align:middle line:90%
in our simple example--

01:16:39.870 --> 01:16:46.530 align:middle line:84%
in our simple two-bidder
uniform example.

01:16:46.530 --> 01:16:52.190 align:middle line:90%


01:16:52.190 --> 01:16:56.990 align:middle line:84%
Well, again, if we look
for a symmetric equilibrium

01:16:56.990 --> 01:17:00.670 align:middle line:84%
where if your value is 0, you
bid nothing and beta is strictly

01:17:00.670 --> 01:17:02.510 align:middle line:84%
increasing, then
our two assumptions

01:17:02.510 --> 01:17:04.470 align:middle line:84%
of revenue equivalence
are going to hold.

01:17:04.470 --> 01:17:07.390 align:middle line:90%
And therefore, we know--

01:17:07.390 --> 01:17:12.790 align:middle line:84%
so revenue equivalence
tells us that T1 hat of v1

01:17:12.790 --> 01:17:14.860 align:middle line:90%
equals v1 squared over 2.

01:17:14.860 --> 01:17:17.110 align:middle line:84%
That's still going to be
true as long as the good goes

01:17:17.110 --> 01:17:18.790 align:middle line:84%
to the bidder who
values it most,

01:17:18.790 --> 01:17:21.830 align:middle line:84%
and the bidder with
0 value pays nothing.

01:17:21.830 --> 01:17:30.610 align:middle line:84%
But now from the auction
format, what is T1 hat of v1?

01:17:30.610 --> 01:17:33.670 align:middle line:90%


01:17:33.670 --> 01:17:34.170 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:17:34.170 --> 01:17:38.638 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: That's the probability
of winning times the--

01:17:38.638 --> 01:17:40.430 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Exactly, so
this should be my bid

01:17:40.430 --> 01:17:45.950 align:middle line:84%
is beta of v1 times the
probability that I win,

01:17:45.950 --> 01:17:46.930 align:middle line:90%
which is v1.

01:17:46.930 --> 01:17:50.070 align:middle line:90%


01:17:50.070 --> 01:17:51.550 align:middle line:90%
So now we set these equal.

01:17:51.550 --> 01:17:52.730 align:middle line:90%
And indeed, we get--

01:17:52.730 --> 01:17:57.190 align:middle line:90%


01:17:57.190 --> 01:17:58.490 align:middle line:90%
which we already derived.

01:17:58.490 --> 01:18:00.755 align:middle line:90%
So we can go back and forth.

01:18:00.755 --> 01:18:02.130 align:middle line:84%
We can solve for
the equilibrium.

01:18:02.130 --> 01:18:04.130 align:middle line:84%
We can see that revenue
equivalence is satisfied.

01:18:04.130 --> 01:18:05.922 align:middle line:84%
Or we can jump right
to revenue equivalence

01:18:05.922 --> 01:18:08.210 align:middle line:84%
and use that together with
the rules of the auction

01:18:08.210 --> 01:18:10.430 align:middle line:84%
to try to derive what
the equilibrium bidding

01:18:10.430 --> 01:18:11.650 align:middle line:90%
behavior will be.

01:18:11.650 --> 01:18:13.190 align:middle line:84%
And this is useful
when we confront

01:18:13.190 --> 01:18:15.270 align:middle line:84%
new auctions we haven't
looked at before because

01:18:15.270 --> 01:18:17.670 align:middle line:84%
of how general revenue
equivalence is.

01:18:17.670 --> 01:18:21.830 align:middle line:84%
I want to end with maybe
one caveat about revenue

01:18:21.830 --> 01:18:22.850 align:middle line:90%
equivalence, though.

01:18:22.850 --> 01:18:28.070 align:middle line:90%


01:18:28.070 --> 01:18:31.830 align:middle line:84%
And revenue equivalence
is often misinterpreted.

01:18:31.830 --> 01:18:33.810 align:middle line:84%
When people interpret
revenue equivalence,

01:18:33.810 --> 01:18:35.790 align:middle line:84%
they often say it's about the
equivalence between auction

01:18:35.790 --> 01:18:36.410 align:middle line:90%
formats.

01:18:36.410 --> 01:18:38.430 align:middle line:84%
They say something like,
the first price-auction

01:18:38.430 --> 01:18:40.970 align:middle line:84%
generates the same revenue
as the second-price auction.

01:18:40.970 --> 01:18:42.550 align:middle line:84%
But if we want to
be really precise,

01:18:42.550 --> 01:18:44.590 align:middle line:84%
revenue equivalence
is a comparison

01:18:44.590 --> 01:18:46.630 align:middle line:84%
between an equilibrium
of one auction

01:18:46.630 --> 01:18:48.450 align:middle line:84%
and an equilibrium
of another auction.

01:18:48.450 --> 01:18:51.670 align:middle line:90%
So a caveat, it's not about--

01:18:51.670 --> 01:18:58.670 align:middle line:84%
so it's not about
the auction per se.

01:18:58.670 --> 01:19:01.690 align:middle line:84%
It's about a particular
equilibrium of the auction.

01:19:01.690 --> 01:19:11.790 align:middle line:90%


01:19:11.790 --> 01:19:14.450 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll say BNE
of the auction.

01:19:14.450 --> 01:19:19.750 align:middle line:90%


01:19:19.750 --> 01:19:23.400 align:middle line:84%
And in fact-- we'll give some
examples-- in a given auction

01:19:23.400 --> 01:19:25.840 align:middle line:84%
format, you could have
multiple equilibria

01:19:25.840 --> 01:19:28.198 align:middle line:84%
that generate different
revenue for the auctioneer

01:19:28.198 --> 01:19:29.740 align:middle line:84%
if those conditions
aren't satisfied.

01:19:29.740 --> 01:19:33.480 align:middle line:84%
So let's make sure we
understand these conditions

01:19:33.480 --> 01:19:37.440 align:middle line:84%
that the bidder with the
lowest value pays nothing,

01:19:37.440 --> 01:19:40.280 align:middle line:84%
and the good is allocated to the
bidder with the highest value.

01:19:40.280 --> 01:19:43.040 align:middle line:84%
These are not properties
simply of the auction format.

01:19:43.040 --> 01:19:45.440 align:middle line:84%
They're properties
of the equilibrium

01:19:45.440 --> 01:19:47.160 align:middle line:84%
together with the
auction format.

01:19:47.160 --> 01:19:49.780 align:middle line:84%
If everyone used a
different bidding strategy,

01:19:49.780 --> 01:19:51.813 align:middle line:90%
then this may no longer be true.

01:19:51.813 --> 01:19:53.480 align:middle line:84%
So these assumptions
are not assumptions

01:19:53.480 --> 01:19:54.980 align:middle line:90%
about the rules of the auction.

01:19:54.980 --> 01:19:57.680 align:middle line:84%
They're assumptions about
a particular equilibrium

01:19:57.680 --> 01:19:58.740 align:middle line:90%
of the auction.

01:19:58.740 --> 01:20:00.620 align:middle line:84%
And whenever we apply
revenue equivalence,

01:20:00.620 --> 01:20:02.912 align:middle line:84%
we're not just comparing the
revenue from two auctions.

01:20:02.912 --> 01:20:05.120 align:middle line:84%
We're saying, let's look
at equilibrium of one

01:20:05.120 --> 01:20:07.940 align:middle line:84%
of this auction and
equilibrium two of auction two,

01:20:07.940 --> 01:20:10.720 align:middle line:84%
and let's compare
the revenue there.

01:20:10.720 --> 01:20:12.100 align:middle line:84%
So that's kind of
a final caveat.

01:20:12.100 --> 01:20:14.548 align:middle line:84%
And we'll see some
implications of that next week.

01:20:14.548 --> 01:20:15.340 align:middle line:90%
So I'll stop there.

01:20:15.340 --> 01:20:17.030 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

01:20:17.030 --> 01:20:20.000 align:middle line:90%